\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Asia Central Banks Tap  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves To Support Their  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2011\par 07:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Official reserves in Indonesia, Philippines, South Korea, Singapore fall in September\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to slow \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' slide aimed at reassuring markets\par \par \uc2 -- Reserve levels remain ample to guard against future shocks\par \par \uc2 By Martin Vaughan\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Several Asian central banks dipped into their vast \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in September to stem sharp declines in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit by global market turbulence driven by the euro-zone's debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 While Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have stabilized this week, the authorities still have ample firepower to support them if global appetite for risky assets takes another turn for the worse, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The scale of the declines in reserves suggests most central banks were trying to smooth volatile movements in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than taking a no-holds-barred approach to defend specific levels.\par \par \uc2 "What they are doing is just trying to reassure markets: 'Just because we have a slip doesn't mean everyone has to pile out and put their money in dollars,'" said Robert Ryan, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "They're saying, 'You don't need to run for the exits, we will be there.'"\par \par \uc2 Reserves held by Indonesia, Southeast Asia's biggest economy, fell by $10.1 billion to $114.5 billion as of Sept. 30 from a month earlier, Bank Indonesia said Friday, reflecting \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the rupiah's tumble as foreign investors pulled capital out of the country.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners cut their holdings of rupiah-denominated government bonds by 12% in September, a pace on par with a selloff in October 2008 at height of the global financial crisis, DBS says.\par \par \uc2 The Philippines' reserves declined by a more modest $301 million to $75.639 billion. Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas attributed the drop to debt payments made by the government and losses from a revaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gold holdings. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been suspected by traders of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as recently as Tuesday to support the peso.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves fell by $15.56 billion to $233.62 billion at the end of September. The reason for the decline was unclear, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore doesn't comment on reasons for changes in official reserves.\par \par \uc2 South Korea reported Wednesday its reserves fell by $8.8 billion to $303.3 billion at the end of September, the sharpest monthly fall since November 2008. Analysts attributed the drop to both won-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a fall in the euro, which hit the value of its euro-denominated assets in dollar terms.\par \par \uc2 Even after the declines in September, reserves remain more than ample to guard against shocks, economists said. Indonesian reserves are sufficient to cover 6.5 months of imports and government debt payments, said Bank Indonesia deputy governor Hartadi Sarwono.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  swooned in September as foreign investors pared holdings in emerging markets and snapped up dollars on worries European banks were vulnerable and the global economy was tipping toward recession. Many have regained some ground this week as European officials have shown renewed resolve to support banks in the region and help Greece get its debt problem under control.\par \par \uc2 But analysts see room for the dollar to rise more in the short-term, as appetite for riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is vulnerable to negative signals from Europe and bad news out of the U.S., which some say could be headed for a recession.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia has been among the most aggressive in defending its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We think Indonesia's September foreign reserves data reveals an investor-friendly policy shift," ING chief Asia economist Tim Condon said. "Bank Indonesia will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to prevent maxi-devaluations."\par \par \uc2 -By Martin Vaughan, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 6415 4033; martin.vaughan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Andreas Ismar in Jakarta, Cris Larano in Manila, In-Soo Nam in Seoul and Gaurav Raghuvanshi contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020111010e7aa002bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
