\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Funds keep slashing bets on Korean won, Singapore dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 972 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2011\par 04:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Range of funds keep cutting \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in Asia ex-Japan\par \par \uc2 * Leveraged, macro funds sell Singapore dollar\par \par \uc2 * Real money funds, bond outflows hit won\par \par \uc2 * Asian c.banks buy own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less\par \par \uc2 (Updates text, prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sept 19 (Reuters) - The South Korean won on Monday fell to its lowest against the yen since March 2009 and the Singapore dollar saw the biggest single-day loss since early August, with a broad range of funds pulling money out of emerging Asia on fears of a Greek default.\par \par \uc2 Asian authorities stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and sold some of their big piles of U.S. dollar reserves to keep their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling too rapidly, having spent billions of dollars since last Wednesday, traders estimated.\par \par \uc2 Leveraged investors, macro-driven funds and interbank speculators sold the Singapore dollar, and offshore institutional investors were the main players dumping the won.\par \par \uc2 After months of resilience, emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have quickly given way over the past week, led by moves in offshore forwards.\par \par \uc2 The position reduction and in some cases liquidation was by no means focused only on Asia, with the 1-month non-deliverable forward for dollar/Brazilian real on Friday hitting the highest since November 2010.\par \par \uc2 Still, the degree of the selloff in Asia has surprised traders and analysts as the relatively strong underlying fundamentals in the region have not changed much. Strategists were busy recommending trades that anticipated further weakness in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We are positioning for further softness in Asia ex-Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What stands out in the current environment is the Hong Kong dollar... it makes sense to use the HKD to remain long against shorting the rest of AXJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  - including the AUD and NZD," said Suresh Kumar Ramanathan, regional rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for CIMB Investment Bank in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities are expected to keep buying their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease volatility, though would only slow the move lower in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than draw a clear line in the sand, especially since weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ultimately help their exports.\par \par \uc2 "Asian central banks are allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness as a buffer, to some degree, to economic headwinds as a way of easing monetary conditions," said Sacha Tihanyi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Scotia Capital in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar weakened past a 200-day moving average, shedding 1.4 percent against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar may weaken more, probably to 1.2626 per dollar, the 50 percent Fibonacci retracement of its strength between November last year and late July, if it ends the day softer than the 200-day moving average of 1.2491.\par \par \uc2 WON\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won hit a six-month low against the dollar on real money funds' selling and touched its weakest in two and a half years against the yen.\par \par \uc2 The won also came under pressure from bond outflows, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were spotted buying the won, especially when it weakened past 1,120 per dollar, but investors took the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as chances to sell the won on rallies, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Some dealers say authorities may not actively cap the won's falls.\par \par \uc2 "They may be concerned more about exports amid worries over Europe. They may not waste bullets around this level," said a foreign bank dealer in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted buying the rupiah\par \par \uc2 from 8,800 per dollar to 8,850.\par \par \uc2 "It totally depends on how many dollars the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to give. If it is a small amount, USD/IDR could break 8,900 even today. If BI cannot guard the volatility, it will be exaggerated by short term investors and will drag the IDR even weaker. It will hit the long-term investors eventually," said a Jakarta-based dealer.\par \par \uc2 Another dealer, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been too aggressive, estimated that it sold $250 million to support the rupiah.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 Change on the day at 0715 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        76.70          76.80       +0.13\par  Sing dlr        1.2584         1.2407       -1.41\par  Taiwan dlr      29.685         29.584       -0.34\par  Korean won     1134.65        1112.50       -1.95\par  Baht             30.43          30.35       -0.26\par  Peso             43.45          43.30       -0.35\par  Rupiah         8840.00        8755.00       -0.96\par  Rupee            47.78          47.27       -1.08\par  Ringgit         3.1085         3.0893       -0.62\par  Yuan            6.3861         6.3834       -0.04\par  Change so far in 2011\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        76.70          81.15       +5.80\par  Sing dlr        1.2584         1.2820       +1.88\par  Taiwan dlr      29.685         30.368       +2.30\par  Korean won     1134.65        1134.80       +0.01\par  Baht             30.43          30.14       -0.95\par  Peso             43.45          43.84       +0.90\par  Rupiah         8840.00        9005.00       +1.87\par  Rupee            47.78          44.70       -6.45\par  Ringgit         3.1085         3.0820       -0.85\par  Yuan            6.3861         6.5897       +3.19\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Masayuki Kitano, Lee Kyoung-ho in SEOUL, Reuters \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analyst Rick Lloyd and Catherine Tan of IFR Markets; Editing by Richard Borsuk)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the new Reuters scrolling global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  service please click\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Double click on brackets for following items: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asia \tab \par Malaysian ringgit   Indonesian rupiah  \tab \par Singapore dollar    Thai baht          \tab \par Taiwan dollar       Hong Kong dollar   \tab \par Philippine peso      Korean won         \tab \par SPEED GUIDES  \tab \par Exotic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      Non-G7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   \