\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/MarketBeat: Singapore's Second Quarter Disappoints\b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2011\par 07:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Market Beat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Matthew Allen and Philip Vahn\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said its Singapore second-quarter report card had 18 disappointments versus 11 positive surprises, with the remaining 27 results in line with expectations.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, [second-quarter] net earnings for these companies fell 3% on-year," it said. This was "despite a 15% on-year jump in revenue as net margins decreased from 10.8% in [the second quarter of 2010] to 9.3% in [the second quarter of 2011]."\par \par \uc2 After this "poor" results season, the house has lowered its 2011 fiscal year market earnings estimate by 4.5%.\par \par \uc2 Among the second quarter's positives, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said six came from the offshore marine sector, which has underperformed defensives by 16% in share-price terms on recession concerns, while dividends rose 13% from last year.\par \par \uc2 "While the overall reporting season is disappointing, the key question for investors is whether this is already priced in. Even post-downgrades, we are looking for 3% [earnings per share] growth in [fiscal year 2011] while the [Straits Times Index] is down 11% in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms and down 5% in U.S. dollar terms so far this year."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Singapore dollar's rise is posing a challenge for exporters but it will be premature for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore to ease its current exchange-rate policy in the upcoming policy review, DBS Group Research said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Apart from diluting export competitiveness, the stronger Singapore dollar has created a negative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  effect on the export numbers, the house says, adding that local exporters are now getting less in Singapore dollar terms unless they have hedged their U.S. dollar exposure.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar rose by about 12% against the greenback over the past year. Still, the house warns that inflation remains a concern in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and any drastic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to soften the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar on the back of the strong capital inflows will prove to be a costly affair for the authority.\par \par \uc2 "Stability is probably the best option in times of volatility," it cautions.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar was trading at 1.2064 Thursday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-18-11 0114ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110818e78i0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
