\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Rubber Settles Lower; Eyes On Yen As Japan  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 12:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Asian rubber settled lower Thursday, with the weakening yen not giving much upside support to the bellwether Tokyo Commodity Exchange, as macro-economic concerns put downward pressure on prices.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark January Tocom natural rubber settled Y0.6 lower at Y390.5 a kilogram, even as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold the yen for the first time since March, while the Bank of Japan announced additional monetary easing, coordinating efforts to stem export-damaging rises in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tocom rubber is usually boosted when the yen weakens as this makes it more competitive than the dollar-denominated physical commodity.\par \par \uc2 "It's about time the Japanese government did something about the yen, but it may already be priced in," said a Hat Yai-based trader at a major exporter.\par \par \uc2 "Tocom players are nervous about setting up new positions with the volatility in yen today [Thursday]," Kaname Gokon, Tokyo-based deputy general manager at commodity brokerage \uc2 Okato Shoji Co\uc2 , said, adding that traders are watching the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for further cues.\par \par \uc2 Tocom will make a break upward with resistance at Y396.5/kg, if USD/JPY breaks above Y80 decisively. Otherwise, Tocom will likely continue to trade in a Y380-Y395/kg range, Gokon said.\par \par \uc2 Japan's finance minister hinted Thursday that the government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary, saying its efforts to weaken the yen are not complete and he will "do what is deemed necessary."\par \par \uc2 Asked about the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far, Yoshihiko Noda said: "We are still halfway through, so I'll sum up the results after this is over."\par \par \uc2 January Tocom rubber closed Y2.6 higher at Y393.1/kg in the night session as the yen weakened further. The night session is considered part of the next trading day.\par \par \uc2 Natural rubber on the Shanghai Futures Exchange settled 0.3% lower, taking cues from SHFE metals amid persistent concerns over the health of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Physical rubber prices were mostly lower in fairly slow trade. Incoming bid prices were too low to do deals, a Thai trader said.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Asian Rubber Futures \par                          Aug 04             Change from \par                                             previous settlement \par Tocom    Jan  RSS3    Y390.5/kg             Down Y0.6 \par Shanghai Jan  SCR5    CNY35,605/ton         Down CNY170 \par Thai     Mar  RSS3    THB148.5/kg           Down THB0.5 \par Sicom    Sep  RSS3    483 cents/kg          Down 1.3 cents \par Sicom    Sep TSR20    471.8 cents/kg        Down 0.2 cents \par  \par Physical prices in Asia, quoted in U.S. cents/kg, free on board: \par  \par  \par Grade  Shipment     Aug 04                  Aug 03 \par                  Bids     Offers       Bids     Offers \par RSS3   Sep/Oct    -       485-490       -       486-490 \par STR20  Sep/Oct    -       484-485       -       484-488 \par SIR20  Sep/Oct    -       472-473       -       474-476 \par SMR20  Sep/Oct    -       476           -       480 \par  \par USS3   Aug       THB131.29-THB132.19/kg THB131.32-THB133.45/kg \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Huileng Tan, Dow Jones Newswires, +65 6415 4083; huileng.tan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Yue Li in Shanghai contributed to this article