\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Lower Late, Stays In Tight Range; Bonds Fall\b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 10:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                        Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD               1.2622   +0.0013 \par  Overnight Rate        0.13%    -7 bps \par  2-Year Bond Yield     0.46%    +1 bp \par  10-Year Bond Yield    2.54%    +4 bps \par  2-Year Swap Offer     0.93%    +5 bps \par  10-Year Swap Offer    3.01%    +11 bps \par  2-10-Year Swap Curve  208 bps  +6 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was down a touch in late Asian trade Wednesday against the U.S. dollar, as markets reacted to comments by Federal Reserve officials that the Fed might wind down its easing earlier than expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 But the impact of those comments on the Singapore dollar was milder than on some other dollar crosses. The U.S. dollar traded at S$1.2622 late Wednesday, up from S$1.2612 when Asian trading opened.\par \par \uc2 Increasing risk appetite, seen in the strong performance of equities during the session, points to support for the Singapore dollar against the greenback in the near-term.\par \par \uc2 "Equity markets are stabilizing, and that's helping Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the Singapore dollar," said a dealer at a foreign bank. The U.S. dollar is still trading "with a heavy tone, and will test again its lows over the coming sessions" against the local unit, the dealer said.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar remained relatively range-bound in Wednesday's trade, even as the greenback recovered some ground against the euro, yen, and Swiss franc on the hawkish Fed comments. The U.S. dollar has traded in a tight range against the local unit all week, from S$1.2604-1.2644.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore unit is hemmed in on the one hand by an improving risk outlook and inflation projections, which would lead to Singapore dollar strength, and on the other hand by a reluctance by traders to push the greenback much lower than S$1.2600 for fear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to one \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist.\par \par \uc2 The rise in the local Straits Times equities index, up 1.7% as of 0800 GMT, helped push Singapore government bonds lower, as bond investors sought higher yields.\par \par \uc2 -By Martin Vaughan; +65 6415 4033; martin.vaughan@dowjones.com [ 30-03-11 0802GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110330e73u000w5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
