\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Too Much At Stake For Japan  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  To Fail-BNP\b0\par\par\uc2 1,820 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2308 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the precarious state of Japan's public finances, the MOF must ensure its strategy to weaken JPY succeeds, say \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists. The USD/JPY is at 81.035. "Over the next few weeks, the challenge will be to ensure that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  succeeds in convincing investors that a floor has been set and that USD/JPY has only one way to go--and that is up. At that stage the combination of investor cash and the BoJ's additional liquidity will drive the pair significantly higher." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2307 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is likely to fall due to profit-taking after the index gained 7.2% over the past two sessions on expectations that Japan will be able to contain its nuclear crisis. "There are no further major developments (on the nuclear front) so it is difficult to be more bullish about buying," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets, adding that losses will, however, likely be limited due to continued buying of undervalued shares by foreign investors. He pegs today's index range at 9500-9650 vs Tuesday's close of 9608.32 (up 4.4%). With details beginning to come out on the impact of the massive March 11 earthquake and tsunami that struck Japan, he adds that trading of individual shares will be based on perceived medium-term impact on earnings. Nikkei 225 June futures ended Chicago trading at 9545 vs the Osaka close at 9530. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2301 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD's recovery from its post-Japan-earthquake lows suggests the pair could have another run at the 1.0250 region, though there could be "sticky" resistance before then, says a dealer. "This 1.0120/30 area should continue to be sticky initially and those thinking that the bounce from 0.9705 lows is now a bit overdone will be looking to sell into it. Alternatively if the move extends further then resistance in the 1.0160 to 1.0260 region should slow the advance...this area has capped the AUD/USD on four occasions over the past five months, so should be strong again if tested." The pair is at 1.009. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index may dip about 0.5% to test the 5-day moving average around 4622.0 amid focus on offshore factors including the 0.4% fall on the S&P 500, the 1.8% rise in oil prices and renewed concern about European sovereign debt problems. The Australian dollar hit a 6-day high in the face of such concerns overnight, so offshore investors don't appear too concerned. But given that the domestic equity market didn't attract much buying interest Tuesday, it may need to fall a bit further before locating some of the eager buying that was evident below 4625.0 on Monday. Investors appear to be waiting for bargains in the current uncertain macro environment. \uc2 David Jones (DJS.AU) \uc2 may get a lift after reaffirming its earnings guidance. But BHP (BHP.AU) looks set to anchor the market after its ADRs fell to A$43.88 vs the local close at A$44.20. The S&P/ASX index last 4643.4. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore's February CPI likely rose 5.5% on-year, at the same pace as in January, according to the median forecast of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones. The continued high reading may prompt the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten again at its April review, economists say. "While inflation looks increasingly broad-based as service costs start creeping higher within the CPI basket, Chinese New Year demand is likely to have been another factor driving up the prices of food and services such as personal care services and travel packages in February," says Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew. February's CPI likely rose 0.4% on-month seasonally-adjusted, slower than the 1.3% rise in January, according to a median estimate of five economists. On-month, the measure likely eased because of lower prices for certificates of entitlement to own vehicles, another economist says. The CPI data are due at 0500 GMT. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Industrial output likely grew 17.98% in February from a year earlier, slightly faster than January's 17.19% increase, according to the median forecast of nine economists in a Dow Jones survey. \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  economist Tomo Kinoshita says said he doesn't expect industrial output to have been affected much by the Lunar New Year holiday, and projected that Taiwan's manufacturing growth would continue at its current pace in the coming months as the U.S. recovery continues and Chinese demand remains robust. The data is due at 0800 GMT. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia's consumer price index likely rose 2.6% on-year in February, according to the median forecast from a Dow Jones poll of 11 economists. In January, the index rose 2.4% on-year. "The accelerating inflation environment, not just in Malaysia but also the broader region, will keep the market focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. Inflation will remain a key challenge for Asia, including Malaysia, in 2011 and 2012," Alvin Liew, an economist at Singapore-based Standard Chartered Bank says. He adds, the robust domestic demand and higher wage costs in Malaysia are also likely put upward price pressures on other major categories in the CPI basket. The data are scheduled for release at 0900 GMT. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2249 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets are likely to trade in relatively tight ranges; there's not much of a cue from Wall Street, and not much in the way of significant data due. Investors are likely to keep an eye on developments surrounding the troubled Japanese nuclear reactors and the conflict in Libya. In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets the USD could remain pressured lower against the EUR and the GBP by growing expectations of rate hikes from the ECB and BOE; JPY traders are likely to remain cautious about pushing down the USD/JPY pair as they remain on watch for possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from Japanese authorities. The EUR/USD is at 1.4194, vs 1.4195 in late NY trade Tuesday, the EUR/JPY is at 114.97 vs 114.95, the USD/JPY is at 81.00 vs 80.95, and GBP/USD is at 1.6371 vs 1.6378. On the data slate there's Singapore's February CPI at 0500 GMT, Taiwan's February industrial output at 0800 GMT, and Vietnam's March CPI and trade. Later there's the BOE's MPC meeting minutes, and the U.K. Budget statement, the E.U.'s industrial new orders and FCCI flash consumer confidence, while from the U.S., there's MBA mortgage applications, new residential sales, the EIA oil report, Dallas Fed president Fisher speaks and Fed chairman Bernanke speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2237 GMT [Dow Jones] Yesterday's spot gold trade was choppy, with the metal moving as high as $1,432.15/oz in the first hour of European trade before dropping to $1,419.30/oz in the early afternoon, but the metal has crept up fairly consistently since then and was last at $1,428.20/oz, up 56 cents from its informal open at 2200 GMT. Geopolitical tensions over Libya and uncertainty over the direction for Japan's earthquake and nuclear disaster-stricken economy aided safe-haven plays, while the U.S. dollar's drop close to its lowest levels in 14 months underpinned the value of the USD-denominated metal; spot gold's biggest drop came as the EUR/USD weakened by 0.4% in a couple of hours around midday. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees the metal breaking to the upside, looking for a close above Monday's $1,435/oz peak to confirm a retest of its record high: "Our targets beyond there are at $1,465/oz and then the $1,500/oz area." Research group GFMS argues the price will average $1,470/oz over the year thanks to uncertainty and low interest rates, but Standard Bank sees prospects of a fall in recent better news from Japan: "Any strong indications that Japan's nuclear crisis might be coming to an end, could see safe-haven demand come off slightly, which would limit the upside." (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2227 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  says ICE Brent crude oil will average $109 in 2011, up $11 on previous forecasts, partly reflecting an extended shutdown of Libyan oil production and widespread unrest in the Middle East and North Africa. The geopolitical risk premium baked into the oil price, estimated at $15-20 currently, will gradually ease after the second quarter. SocGen also cuts estimates of OPEC crude spare capacity. For February, OPEC spare capacity was estimated at 3.1-3.6 million bbl/d, of which 2.6-3.1 million was in Saudi Arabia. "The Japanese earthquake is not a game changer for oil. Some incremental oil demand is expected from reconstruction and from oil-fired power generation. However, in the broader context, this is neutral for crude and possibly already priced in for products," SocGen adds. ICE Brent crude for May delivery settled Tuesday up 74 cents, or 0.6%, at $115.70/bbl; Nymex crude for May delivery is now down 3 cents at $104.94/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2223 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian banks are likely to outperform resources for the next few months and could lift the S&P/ASX 200 to 5000, say \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  traders. They expect a 4500-5000 range for the next five months, with "potentially another big fall ahead of us in May when markets realize there will be no (more) quantitative easing (in the U.S.)," although U.S. economic recovery is exected to "backstop" the market. Banks are expected to find dividend-related buying before their upcoming reporting season. S&P/ASX 200 last 4643.4. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 1908ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110322e73m000fk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
