\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Lower; Suspected  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Limits Fall\b0\par\par\uc2 1,643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2011\par 02:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is at 30.60 from 30.66 late Wednesday in Asia in tandem with broad USD weakness. Continuous portfolio inflows amid recovering Thai shares are also weighing on the pair. However, suspected USD-supportive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at around the 30.60 level is helping to limit the pair's fall, two dealers says. "The overall sentiment is pressuring the dollar/baht. I think the BOT must have absorbed at least a few hundred million dollars already this morning," one dealer says. If the 30.60 level fails to hold, next support is pegged at 30.55, while resistance is at 30.65. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI rises 0.2% to 1508.58 with some profit-taking capping further gains, says a local brokerage dealer. "The market appears to be in a consolidation mode, trading within a very tight three-point range since the opening bell on a lack of major catalysts," says the dealer; he tips immediate support at 1500, and resistance at 1510. Market breadth is positive with gainers outnumbering losers 189 to 139, on a volume of 239 million shares worth MYR115 million. HwangDBS Vickers Research says technically, the fact that the index closed off its 1495 support Wednesday, suggests that the benchmark may recover a bit more today. "But its immediate upside potential is expected to be restricted by profit-taking activity," says the house. Among gainers, Kulim (2003.KU) is 1.8% higher at MYR14.64, MMHE (5186.KU) is up 1.6% at MYR6.54, and CIMB (1023.KU) climbs 0.7% to MYR8.16. On the downside, SP Setia (8664.KU) falls 1.1% to MYR6.23, Proton (5304.KU) eases 0.9% to MYR4.22, and Carlsberg (2836.KU) slips 0.8% to MYR6.25. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is lower at 8,880 from 8,890 late Wednesday as inflows continue, dealers say. "The dollar-rupiah seems to be taking its cue from the dollar's weakness against most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer says. The pair's slide, however, is slowed by demand from state banks, raising speculation that they may have bought on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Bank Indonesia earlier this month indicated it would allow the local unit to appreciate to help fight inflation; "if it's true that Bank Indonesia bought the dollar, I think it did it only to smoothen the volatility, not to support the dollar at a certain level," the dealer says. The pair is tipped to trade in a 8,870-8,895 range. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is off 0.2% at 3089.52, with 189 million shares traded worth S$331 million, as the positive U.S. cue and upbeat regional markets fail to stir the local market from its consolidating pattern after last week's sharp fall. Phillip Securities says the STI's close Wednesday tells of some buying coming in, adding that while a test of 3120 might be possible today, "we want to see price maintain a close above 3120 for us to begin shifting the short term bias back upwards." The house tips support at 3080, then 3065 before 3055/3050, with resistance at 3116/3120 before 3150. It adds, there's "not much visibility at this point. We note that 3120 and 3055/3050 are key levels." Keppel (BN4.SG) is up 1.9% at S$11.80 on more order win news flow; NOL (N03.SG) is up 2.8% at S$2.19 after reporting a swing to profits in FY10, and recommending a dividend of 4.6 Singapore cents for 2010. \uc2 Tiger Airways\uc2  (J7X.SG) is the most active so far, down 4.2% at S$1.59. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is set to rise, which would be its 4th session of gains out of 5, tracking gains on Wall Street overnight, but the index is likely to find resistance at its 50-day moving average around 23,400, says Ben Kwong, chief operating officer at \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 . He adds, A-shares' newfound strength is likely to aid HK stocks but the recent fund flow pattern (to developed markets from emerging markets) hasn't shown signs of being reversed, which will cap HK shares' gains. HKEx (0388.HK) may rebound after it underpformed recently on competition concerns due to news of merger between Deutsche Boerse and \uc2 NYSE Euronext\uc2  (NYX). The HSI ended Wednesday +1.1% at 23,156.97. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are expected to rise, tracking gains in U.S. and most Asian markets. The bellweather PSEi is tipped to rise towards 3860 resistance after it ended 0.6% higher at 3812.04 Wednesday; support is pegged at 3750. "The Dow is up while Asian markets are stronger," Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities, adding major blue chip PLDT (TEL.PH) should lead market move as stock is still cheaper than its ADRs in New York. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may rise to 84.20 after the pair hit an eight-week high of 83.98 Wednesday, says Sumino Kamei, senior analyst at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ; "the main scenario is that (the pair) will test a breach of 84.00" later Thursday. For further buying cues, she says investors are focused on data out of the U.S. later Thursday, including U.S. weekly jobless claims, which she notes showed a strong outcome last week, and served as a catalyst for USD's ascent. She adds although the USD/JPY failed to rise above 84.00 overnight, the band around 83.50 looks firm at its downside. The USD/JPY is at 83.58, little changed from 83.69 late Wednesday.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] A \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  survey on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging intentions shows most businesses expect the AUD to remain over 1 US dollar throughout 2011. But the CBA report says it's own forecast is for the AUD to gradually ease and end the year at 0.9200, driven by a firmer USD. The report says if the CBA forecast is correct, importers who are unhedged may face higher import costs. Survey of 600 businesses also reveals a surge in the number of importers planning to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure. CBA says it appears that a growing number of importers are hedging to lock in the benefits of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they believe the AUD/USD will soon peak. CBA adds, the high AUD appears to be taking its toll on exporters with only 39% of exporters expecting to increase their AUD/USD exposure. The AUD/USD is trading at 1.0034. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are likely to fall as heavyweight property developers may come under pressure after the Beijing municipal government yesterday places fresh limits on home purchases. Analysts expect the Shanghai Composite Index to move in a 2900-2940 range after the index ends up 0.9% at 2923.90 yesterday. "The market will likely consolidate for a while before rising again," says Shenyin Wanguo Securities analyst Wei Daoke. On Wednesday, Beijing imposed fresh limits on home purchases, including requiring first-time non-residents home buyers to prove that they have made social security payments for the past five years before they can purchase a home in the city. Property stocks are likely to fall 1%-3% on the news, analysts say. The Shenzhen Composite Index ends up 1.7% to 1283.26 yesterday. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's raising of its inflation forecasts for 2011 earlier, increases the chance that MAS will tighten in April, but non-monetary policy tools could also be used, an analyst from a local bank says. Singapore raised the forecast to 3.0%-4.0% vs 2.0%-3.0% earlier and vs 2.8% in 2010. "The revision isn't a surprise. While noting inflation may hit 5%-6% in the first few months, the MAS is still quite upbeat about (inflation moderating in) the second half," says Selena Ling, an economist at OCBC. She adds "the view on potential higher re-centering (the exchange rate band) is gaining traction, but it's not a 100% watertight case for tightening. There are instruments like labor market policies that are available for them to use." (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP may retreat further as the EUR and regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  advance in early Asian trade against the USD. The pair may slide below 43.50 after settling at 43.585 late Wednesday, with traders pegging support at 43.30 and resistance at 43.70. "We're expecting a stronger peso today. There's evidence of more risk appetite overnight and the dovish Fed statement," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY may consolidate with downward bias, tracking weakness in the USD offshore overnight. The central parity will likely be set near record-low levels of around 6.5850 vs 6.5855 Wednesday, says a Beijing-based local bank trader. The pair is tipped to trade in a 6.5800-6.5900 range. "The past few sessions have seen increased volatility in the fixing that complements China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator's launch of yuan options yesterday," the trader says. (jean.yung@dowjones.com) [ 02-16-11 2056ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110217e72h00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
