\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Hit Multi-Year Highs As Risk Appetite Revives\b0\par\par\uc2 869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2011\par 07:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Hit Multi-Year Highs As Risk Appetite Revives," at 0507 GMT, misstated the time in the 11th paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Arran Scott \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Several Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit multi-year highs against the dollar Tuesday as revived risk appetite encouraged investors to plough money back into the region.\par \par \uc2 A rally in regional share prices, triggered in part by strength on Wall Street overnight and a decline in crude oil prices, set a bullish tone in Asia. Investors appear to be buying into the notion Asian central banks will use a combination of stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and higher interest rates this year to temper inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit and Taiwan dollar rose to their highest levels since October 1997, when the Asian financial crisis sent those and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a tailspin. The South Korean won, which has lagged in the broad Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation last year, struck levels not seen since the \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  collapse in September 2008 clobbered Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Indonesia rupiah added to gains it has made since Friday, when the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates and signaled it would use a stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in inflation that has surged above its target band. The rupiah had been hurt by concerns that Jakarta was falling behind the curve in containing inflation, but has now nearly recovered the losses it had logged since mid-November when such worries began to escalate.\par \par \uc2 Inflation has been picking up in Asia, fueled by fast economic growth, loose monetary policies and soaring prices of food and commodities on global markets. Central banks across the region have increased interest rates and taken steps to soak up liquidity that has gushed into the region as investors hunted for higher returns that were available in low interest-rate environments in the U.S. and Europe.\par \par \uc2 "The final tool in the armory of the region's authorities is \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation," Fiona Lake, an analyst at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , wrote in a research note. "Korea, Indonesia and Taiwan have shown greater desire for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation to help tackle inflation."\par \par \uc2 The won has some catching up to do with Asia's strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the past year: the Singapore dollar, the Malaysian ringgit and the Thai baht. The Bank of Korea has also turned more hawkish in its comments on tackling rising inflation. Economists at Nomura expect the BOK to deliver its first back-to-back 25 basis-point hike at its next meeting Friday, followed by 25 basis points of moves in April and June, lifting the official rate to 3.50% by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 What's more, the U.S. has put the won on its list of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for which "there is room for a greater degree of exchange-rate stability and less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department, in its annual \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  report Friday, cited International Monetary Fund calculations that the won is 5%-20% undervalued. The U.S. likely has more leverage with Korea than with China on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters. Sean Callow, a strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 , points out that Congress hasn't ratified the U.S.-South Korea free trade agreement signed by Presidents Barack Obama and Lee Myung-bak in December.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to KRW1,102.50 from KRW1,107.50 late Monday. It was last at KRW1,104.20.\par \par \uc2 Against the Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at NT$28.9180 at 0500 GMT, after earlier touching NT$28.900, its lowest intraday level since October 1997, on foreign capital flows into Taipei stocks on the first trading day after the Lunar New Year holidays. A trader said the market is on the lookout for the Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is thought to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly near the close of trading to smooth out moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Against the rupiah, the dollar was around IDR8,910, back near the IDR8,890 level at which it traded in mid-November, before concerns over inflation started to permeate the market.\par \par \uc2 The dollar carved out a fresh 13-year low against the ringgit, last trading at MYR3.0330. That was down from MYR3.0360 late Monday, when traders said the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to temper the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. Dealers were on the alert for the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday to curb the ringgit.\par \par \uc2 Against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, which has recently logged record highs, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at SGD1.2734, down from SGD1.2746 late Monday as risk appetite gathers momentum, especially for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said a trader with a foreign bank. "Flows are coming back into Asia mainly because investors are betting on the strong (economic) fundamentals in the region," he said.\par \par \uc2 The baht and Philippine peso were also higher Tuesday, helped by the broader rally in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4042; arran.scott@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Mark Cranfield in Singapore, I Made Sentana in Jakarta, Ankur Relia in Kuala Lumpur, P.R. Venkat in Singapore, and Fanny Liu in Taipei contributed to this article. [ 08-02-11 0616GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110208e728000f8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
