\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: SNB Signals  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Off Agenda -\uc2 Commerzbank\b0\par\par\uc2 1,926 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2011\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0700 GMT (Dow Jones) \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Philipp Hildebrand\f28 \uc2 \f2 's comments Thursday that the risk of deflation has largely disappeared suggests \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is now off the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's agenda, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "His remarks that the stability of the euro-zone is decisive for the franc clearly points to the helplessness of the SNB," according to analyst Ulrich Leuchtmann. EUR/CHF at 1.3038 by 0651 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS steady despite surging funding rates, as traders expect liquidity conditions to improve after the Lunar New Year holiday in early February. The 1-year IRS is up 1 bps at 3.55%-3.63%, the 5-year IRS is flat at 4.06%-4.15%. "We all know demand for money will decline after the Chinese New Year, not to mention the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is injecting lots of liquidity into the market," says a Shanghai-based local bank trader. The PBOC is injecting CNY249 billion by suspending this week's open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, but the benchmark 7-day repo is still 137 bps up at 7.48%. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi ends down 1.7% at 2069.92, extending yesterday's 0.4% loss. The index posted its largest daily loss in more than two months on the back of growing concerns that China will take further tightening measures in the near term, which stoked foreign net selling for profit-taking, analysts say. "There's also a possibility that foreign investors will move their liquidity from emerging stocks to stocks in developed countries," says \uc2 Kiwoom Securities\uc2  market strategist Ma Ju-ok. However, he adds, China worries aren't likely to weigh the Kospi much longer, considering the rebound in Shanghai stocks today. He tips a 2050-2150 range for next week. Most large-cap stocks were down on profit-taking; \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) ended off 1.1% at KRW971,000, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) ended down 4.4% at KRW185,500 and \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) closed 2.9% lower at KRW477,500. But \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) rose 1.2% to KRW122,000 on hopes that its 4Q earnings will top market expectations. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 0.4% at 23,915.63, with falls led by commodity stocks, such as Cnooc (0883.HK) which is down 1.9% at HK$18.66 after crude price weakness. Ben Kwong, COO at KGI says inflation concerns in China and interest rate hike worries are expected to weigh on the stock market in the near term. He expects the index to have its 20-day moving average (23,617) as a near-term support, compared with its early low of 23,878.38. \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  (0293.HK) is down 5.2% at HK$21.10, likely due to profit-taking pressure after the stock's recent outperformance, as it rose 58.9% over the past 12 months, compared with the HSI's 12.8% gain in the same period. But some HK properties are higher, as China tightening concerns have less impact on this sector; Cheung Kong (0001.HK) is +0.6% at HK$133.70 and \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK) is +1.2% at HK$35.10. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] Chinese refined copper imports decline on-month and on-year in December after a recovery in November as downstream users were worried by the rise of copper prices last month and are stocking up in anticipation of prices coming down. Analysts say the firmly-closed arbitrage opportunity between Shanghai and London also contributed to the fall in December imports; they add, imports may remain sluggish in January and February as most players wind down for the Lunar New Year holiday which starts Feb. 2, but Chinese market participants will return to the market to buy after the Lunar New Year holiday due to seasonality, by which will see an uptick in refined copper shipments from overseas. China's refined copper imports in December fall 6.5% on-year, slide 1.6% on-month to 228,609 tons; 2010 refined copper imports at 2.92 million tons, down 8.4% on-year, Customs data showed. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is down 0.4% at 3192.60, with selling in banks, commodity plays and rig builders weighing on the index as caution prevails after yesterday's sharp fall. Volume falls from yesterday, and is at 1.01 billion shares so far. DBS Vickers strategist Yeo Kee Yan says "earnings season has a history of being choppy. Back in December I highlighted that the market should start to rise, but that profit-taking might kick in by mid-January, which is actually what's happening now. Ultimately I believe that corporate earnings will support the market for blue chips, so downside will be limited in the near term." Below 3200, he tips support at 3165. He adds, other than profit-taking, traders may also be paring down holdings ahead of the Lunar New Year holiday (Feb. 3). Noble (N21.SG) is down 1.3% at S$2.24, OCBC (O39.SG) is off 0.6% at S$9.94, Golden Agri (E5H.Sg) is down 2.0% at S$0.725, and Keppel (BN4.SG) is down 1.7% at S$11.30. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is slightly higher at 7.7862 from 7.7849 earlier Friday, compared with 7.7817 late Thursday. "I see a U.K. bank buying the U.S. dollar with a size around US$50 million," a senior banker at a Chinese bank says. He believes the pair is likely to stay high next week with strong corporate demand from note-issuing banks before the Lunar New Year. He tips the pair to trade in tight 7.7845-7.7865 range for the rest of the session. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0630 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei down 1.6%; S&P/ASX down 0.6%; HSI down 0.2%; Kospi down 1.7%; Taiex down 0.8%; Sensex down 0.1%; Shanghai Composite +1.3%; STI down 0.3%; Indonesian shares down 3.3%; Philippine shares down 1.4%; SET down 1.7%; NZX-50 +0.4%; KLCI down 0.9%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the EUR briefly climbed on cues from China shares' gains before retracing its advance; EUR/USD at 1.3491 after trading as high as 1.3514 vs 1.3473 in NY late Thursday, the EUR/JPY is at 111.86, off 112.12 intraday high, vs 111.80, and the USD/JPY is at 82.91 vs 82.98. State-run China Securities Journal says in a commentary China could raise interest rates around the Lunar New Year holiday in early February and strong inflation pressure could push the CPI above 6.0% in part of 1H. Shin Je-yoon, Korea's Deputy Minister of Strategy and Finance for Foreign Affairs, says Korean authorities are not considering further measures to control fund flows into and out of local equity and fixed income markets for now. Japan's annual budget gap could hit Y23.2 trillion at end of fiscal-year March 2021, according to new a government estimate, likely bolstering the case for a sharp tax rise. Mathee Supapongse, Senior Directory of Thailand's Domestic Economy Department, says the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  will probably maintain its current 2011 economic growth forecast of 3.0%-5.0% when it provides its quarterly forecasts of economic indicators later today. New Zealand November adjusted retail sales +1.5% on-month vs October's revised 2.4% decline, +1.2% expected; November retail sales ex-autos fell 0.2% vs October's revised 1.6% decline, +0.5% expected. Australia import price index falls 3.8% on-quarter, export price index falls 8.1% on-quarter. Japan November METI all industry index fell 0.1% on-month vs October's 0.3% decline. Spot gold is at $1346.20/oz, up 60 cents vs the NY close. March Nymex crude oil futures are up 27 cents at $89.86/bbl. (john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds and bond futures are little moved in afternoon trade amid a lack of decisive leads. Some bargain-hunting is kicking in after recent losses, but not in big volumes as overall sentiment remains battered on expectations of more rate hikes from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The 5-year yield is quoted down 1 bp at 4.34%, the 10-year yield is flat at 4.71%, while March futures are up three ticks at 102.51. Regarding the yield curve, "it has been showing either bear-flattening or a bull-steepening movement nowadays," says a bond manager with a securities firm, noting that the yield curve seems biased towards the latter for today. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 March futures close down 1.5% at 10,280 vs their intraday low of 10,250, as speculation grows that China may soon raise interest rates. Foreign players in particular were seen selling, as they viewing the upside as limited amid Japanese institutions' persistently selling in the cash market, says a manager at a Japanese brokerage. He says hedge funds likely began building fresh short positions in the futures index, and added that the contract may fall to support at 10,000 next week. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] European stocks are set to open higher Friday, after U.S. stocks managed to end well off their lows Thursday, says Jonathan Sudaria at Capital Spreads. He's calling London's FTSE 100 to open six points higher at 5874, Frankfurt's DAX to open 13 points higher at 7037 and Paris's CAC-40 to open 15 points higher at 3980. Stocks may also get a lift from the German IFO business climate, due at 0900 GMT, says Sudaria. "While expectations are for the figure to maintain December's level of 109.9, some traders are positioning themselves for a figure well above expectations after the surge in the ZEW economic sentiment earlier this week." In addition to the IFO business climate UK retail sales are due at 0930 GMT. (michele.maatouk@dowjones.com) [ 01-21-11 0200ET ]\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] The FTSE 100 is called to open up 15 points at 5883, according to Ben Potter at IG Markets. This comes after U.S. stocks rebounded into the close Thursday, ending off lows. "UK retail sales data and the German IFO report [at 0930 GMT and 0900 GMT, respectively] could provide some momentum, whilst there's also still a raft of earnings news on the radar, most notably from across the Atlantic with GE, Schlumberger and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  all poised to release numbers before the start of trade," says Potter. As far as UK retail sales are concerned, the data are likely to show a small reversal in December, with spending expected to have fallen 0.2% month-on-month, says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 . (michele.maatouk@dowjones.com) [ 01-21-11 0200ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110121e71l0003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
