\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ MAS: May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Market To Damp  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dollar Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 880 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2010\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 27 Oct 2010 00:00 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  MAS: Sterilizes \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Smooth Monetary Base\par \par \uc2 27 Oct 2010 00:00 EDT *DJ  MAS: \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bank Balances 'Relatively Unaffected' By \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 27 Oct 2010 00:00 EDT *DJ  MAS: \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  2010 GDP On Track To Grow 13%-15%\par \par \uc2 27 Oct 2010 00:00 EDT *DJ  MAS Forecasts Singapore CPI To Rise To 4% By Year-End\par \par \uc2 27 Oct 2010 00:00 EDT *DJ  MAS Forecasts Singapore 2010 CPI At 2.5%-3%, 2011 At 2%-3%\par \par \uc2 27 Oct 2010 00:00 EDT *DJ  Singapore MAS: Growth In Asia Ex-Japan Has Likely Peaked\par \par \uc2 27 Oct 2010 00:00 EDT *DJ  Singapore MAS: Risk of Developed Econs Slipping Into Recession Receded\par \par \uc2 27 Oct 2010 00:00 EDT DJ  MAS: May \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Curb \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  Dollar; Asia Faces Capital-Inflow Risk\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb rises in the local dollar as Asia faces risks from a surge of capital flowing into the region.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, in its annual macroeconomic review, said that it sterilizes its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the city-state's monetary base stable and that its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have little impact on local banks' current accounts.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  has seen strong capital inflows since the second half of 2009 amidst broad-based weakness of the U.S. dollar and the relatively positive outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  economy," the MAS said. "In such instances, MAS may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to dampen upward pressures on the Singapore dollar."\par \par \uc2 It is rare for the MAS to discuss its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . People familiar with the matter told Dow Jones Newswires twice last month that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sold Singapore dollars to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \uc2 The MAS said in its review that its decision this month to tighten monetary policy was based on the assessment that economic activity would remain high and Singapore's economy would keep expanding, though at a slower and more sustainable pace. At the same time, domestic cost pressures have been rising and strong income growth has placed pressure on the prices of some consumption items, it said.\par \par \uc2 The MAS surprised financial markets Oct. 14 by announcing it would guide the trade-weighted Singapore dollar upward at a slightly faster pace and let it rise a bit further from its targeted midpoint.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses the exchange rate, rather than interest rates as its key policy tool because trade flows dwarf the island's tiny domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 The MAS said it tightened because of rising cost pressures. Data released Monday showed that consumer prices rose 3.7% on year in September, the fastest pace in 20 months.\par \par \uc2 In its review the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast CPI inflation will rise close to 4% by the year end and stay high in the first half of 2011 before moderating. Full-year inflation will average between 2.5%-3% in 2010 and 2%-3% next year, the report said.\par \par \uc2 The MAS said the economy is "on track" to grow by 13%-15% in 2010. The estimate matches that of the government.\par \par \uc2 The government said Oct. 14 the economy contracted 19.8% in annualized, seasonally adjusted terms in the third quarter after rocketing 27.3% in the second and a record 45.9% in the first. A stronger-than-expected 26.2% growth in manufacturing output reported Tuesday, however, may lead to an upward revision of third-quarter growth numbers, economists said.\par \par \uc2 Singapore could slip into a technical recession, or two quarters of on-quarter contraction, in the second half of 2010, Senior Minister of State in the Ministry of Trade and Industry and Ministry of Education S. Iswaran said in Parliament Oct. 18.\par \par \uc2 "These short-term adjustments are not indicative of an imminent relapse into broad-based weakness in the economy and the level of economic activity is expected to remain high in the quarters ahead," the MAS report said.\par \par \uc2 On the global economy, the MAS said recovery has lost momentum in recent months and the outlook for Singapore's key trading partners will remain uneven.\par \par \uc2 "After a strong start in the first half of 2010, the global recovery has lost momentum in recent months, partly due to the scaling back of government stimulus packages and the fading of inventory restocking effects," it said. "Thus, while the risk of the developed economies slipping back into recession has generally receded, final demand is likely to remain sluggish.\par \par \uc2 Growth in Asia, which exited the recession earlier, appears to have peaked in the first quarter and has slowed to a more sustainable rate, the MAS said. The pace of growth in trade-dependant economies such as Korea and Taiwan was unsustainable as it came from inventory replenishment arising from a robust demand for information technology products.\par \par \uc2 In comparison, growth has been more stable in the domestic-oriented economies of China, India and Indonesia, underpinned by continued strength in consumption and investment spending, the report said.\par \par \uc2 -By Gaurav Raghuvanshi and P.R. Venkat; Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 154; gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 27, 2010 00:00 ET (04:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101027e6ar000lq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
