\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/PHP Higher Late; 43.05-43.40 Band Tipped Thu\b0\par\par\uc2 1,382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2010\par 10:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher late Wednesday, lifted by broad USD recovery against majors, regionals. Pair at 43.26 from 43.11 late Tuesday. "The market generally tracked the euro although there were some inflows that tempered the USD's rise," says local bank trader. Tips pair likely to stay within 43.05-43.40 range Thursday with market set to track overseas leads. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD hits intraday high 30.900 late vs 30.760 yesterday, boosted by stronger global USD, short-covering by foreign investors amid uncertainties ahead of U.S. Fed meeting, says foreign bank trader. Still, notes downward trend remains intact; "today is only a short-term rebound," she says. Tips pair to fall in 30.700-30.900 band Thursday. She and another local bank trader suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have bought some USD late in session as it had done so recently. Both estimate \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought around US$30 million. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0820 GMT [Dow Jones] Portuguese bonds outperform ahead of Wednesday PGB 3.6% October 2014 and PGB 4.45% August 2018 auctions. Portuguese bonds have benefited in the last week from a growing confidence that the 2011 budget bill will be passed through parliament with government and the opposition seemingly willing to make compromises to ensure the bill goes through, says Orlando Green, Credit Agricole CIB strategist. Investor sentiment in the peripheral markets has improved recently and this has seen a tightening of spreads to German bunds. The 10-year Portuguese/German yield spread is 6 bps tighter on the day at 305 bps, Spanish/German 2.5 bps tighter at 154 bps, Italian/German 2 bps tighter at 129 bps, Irish/German 1.5 bps tighter at 392 bps while Greek/German is 19 bps wider at 735 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0817 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD is lifted by a report in the WSJ that the Fed will only need to buy a "few hundred" billion dollars of Treasury bonds in the next several months rather than resort to the 'shock and awe' QE that many had expected next month. Another report in the FT that G-20 is likely to agree next month to reduce current account imbalances is also providing support for the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The AUD is one of the main losers, falling heavily after Australian CPI comes in a little softer than the market expected and reduces speculation over an early rate hike. The EUR is down at $1.3803, while the USD has risen to Y81.94. The GBP is down at $1.5788 while the AUD is down at $0.9706. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0814 GMT [Dow Jones] The market is keeping a close eye on the ECB's 3-month liquidity tender Wednesday given the recent spike in money market rates, with results due around 0915 GMT. The amount expiring is EUR23B but with 3-month Euribor now above the ECB's 1% refinancing rate, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  feels that a net increase in the LTRO is absolutely needed for tensions to ease. "The next key event will be the expiry of EUR35.7B of 6-month liquidity on Nov. 11 and market participants will be very worried if a larger buffer is not rebuilt ahead of this," SocGen says.(neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher late at 3.1050 vs 3.0950 Tuesday; local dealer says pair rising on USD strength vs emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adds, Bank Negara governor Zeti saying Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent "disorderly" market also causing pair to rise. Tips 3.0950-3.1150 range Thursday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT [Dow Jones] Apart from talk of small scale QE, the USD is also getting support from a report in the FT that the G-20 next month is likely to set targets to reduce current account imbalances, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . The bank argues this will move the focus away from nominal exchange rates. "Reducing foreign imbalances without emphasising on exchange rates requires Asia to promote domestic demand, reduce trade barriers and regulations," the bank says, noting that this all takes pressure off the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty is slightly weaker tracing the weakening of the euro to the dollar, a trader says. Adds the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected mostly stable ahead of the interest rate decision expected later Wednesday, but may become volatile if deliberations are unusually protracted. EUR/PLN is at 3.9420 from 3.9275 late Tuesday, USD/PLN at 2.8560 from 2.8348. Average yield on 2-year bonds is at 4.77% from 4.78%, 5-year at 5.26% from 5.24%, 10-year at 5.68% from 5.66%. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT [Dow Jones] Firmer French household spending could provide a significant offset to the expected drag on GDP coming from net exports, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  economist Tullia Bucco after official data shows a 1.2% rise in French consumer spending on manufactured goods in the third quarter from the second quarter. Says this reinforces upside risks to \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 's 0.3% quarter-on-quarter 3Q GDP forecast. Bucco notes the rebound in September was driven by a "whopping 11.2% surge in car spending," partly correcting the weakness observed in car spending since the spring. (william.horobin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0806 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 14 tad higher at 100.5, 102 bps vs yesterday's indication of 100/102 bps. Market tested psychologically important level of 100 bps earlier, but index bounced back due to bargain-hunting by some players, says CDS trader at major Japan bank. Says market keen to find out whether BOJ willing to buy low-rated corporate debt in planned Y5 trillion asset-purchasing program. BOJ widely expected to announce details of program at conclusion of tomorrow's policy-setting meeting. Says "the index may widen again if the specifics are not bold enough as the market has already it priced in," including potential purchases of BBB-rated corporate bonds. Tips benchmark index to trade in 100-110 bps range in near term. Single name CDS deals include \uc2 Keio Corp\uc2 . (9008.TO) at 27 bps, \uc2 Marubeni Corp\uc2 . (8002.TO) at 100 bps vs 105 bps on Oct. 18.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0758 GMT [Dow Jones] October HSI futures down 1.8% at 23,137, in line with cash market's 1.8% fall, as sentiment turns negative after cash market stuck in 559-point range since it hit its 2-year high of 23,866 (Oct. 14). Contract trading at about 30-point discount to cash, after ending at premium over past 2 sessions, pointing to slightly negative bias for near-term direction. Volume heavy at 111,636 lots, as traders rollover positions ahead of current-month contract's expiry Thursday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0757 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD higher, tracking broad USD strength vs majors, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Wall Street Journal reports size of U.S. quantitative easing likely smaller than previously estimated, Philip Wee of DBS says. Pair last at 1.3032 vs 1.2979 morning. "Expectations of the U.S. quantitative easing have run ahead and the U.S. dollar is now adjusting itself after the WSJ report this morning," Wee says. Trader at international bank says immediate resistance for pair at 1.3050, followed by 1.3100. Support at 1.2950. \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  MAS earlier said in report it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb SGD rise as Asia faces risks of strong capital inflows. "Singapore has seen strong capital inflows since the second half of 2009 amidst broad-based weakness of the U.S. dollar and the relatively positive outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  economy. In such instances, MAS may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to dampen upward pressures on the Singapore dollar," MAS says in October macroeconomic review. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com) [ 10-27-10 0425ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101027e6ar0005k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
