\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/SGD Higher;QE Expectations Have Run Ahead-DBS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2010\par 09:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0757 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD higher, tracking broad USD strength vs majors, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Wall Street Journal reports size of U.S. quantitative easing likely smaller than previously estimated, Philip Wee of DBS says. Pair last at 1.3032 vs 1.2979 morning. "Expectations of the U.S. quantitative easing have run ahead and the U.S. dollar is now adjusting itself after the WSJ report this morning," Wee says. Trader at international bank says immediate resistance for pair at 1.3050, followed by 1.3100. Support at 1.2950. \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  MAS earlier said in report it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb SGD rise as Asia faces risks of strong capital inflows. "Singapore has seen strong capital inflows since the second half of 2009 amidst broad-based weakness of the U.S. dollar and the relatively positive outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  economy. In such instances, MAS may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to dampen upward pressures on the Singapore dollar," MAS says in October macroeconomic review. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0755 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling got a boost Tuesday as stronger than expected UK 3Q GDP data doused the possibility of the BOE announcing quantitative easing at next week's meeting. However, sterling's potential for recovery vs the EUR should be limited as the issue of QE is not entirely off the agenda yet. "The government's savings programme will only reflect itself in the British economy over the coming months. The expectations regarding QE have merely been postponed," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. EUR/GBP now at 0.8748. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT [Dow Jones] Delivering the UK's spending review was an unenviable task, says Wednesday's Taking Stock column, but Chancellor of the Exchequer George Osborne did at least get a lucky break with the timing. (david.cottle@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds lower for 3rd straight session amid ongoing concerns over inflation, following National Bureau of Statistics chief economist Yao Jingyuan's remarks Monday that China's CPI will accelerate in October from September, given low statistical base last year. "We expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will step up its fund-withdrawing efforts via regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming weeks to curb inflationary pressure," says Shenzhen-based trader with local insurer. Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 126.26 vs 126.41 last close, short-term supported tipped at 126.0.(ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD has been hurt by the softer than expected 3Q inflation data but Credit Agricole says it would be cautious about getting overly dovish. The fact that inflation is close to the RBA's mid-point target is encouraging, but the signals on economic growth remain very upbeat, the labor market is tight and the support from China's burgeoning economy remains significant, Credit Agricole says. "A modest shortfall on inflation is not the death-knell for further rate hikes in Australia, nor for renewed gains in the AUD," it adds. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0747 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD's outlook has become more complex, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . The bank suggests the chances of a sustained drop below 1.38 have increased. But, it adds, "as long as 1.3680 holds, buying-on-dips might be favored as sentiment may turn around quickly, once more details on the Fed decision on QE2 become available." The pair is now at 1.3799. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT [Dow Jones] AmResearch says Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to keep rates unchanged at 2.75% at next policy rate meeting on November 12. "With \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  (having) frontloaded rate hikes in the earlier part of the year, we see greater flexibility in keeping to a neutral stance -- in our opinion, no more hike over the next 3-6 months," says economist Manokaran Mottain. Adds, recent data showing inflation moderating to 1.8% in September, from 2.1% in August further supports \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's flexibility in containing any asset bubble in system. Governor Zeti says Tuesday Bank Negara may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but only to prevent "disorderly" market conditions; "we do not think Bank Negara would resort to any more capital controls, as it would certainly affect investor confidence, at a time when (Malaysia is) attempting to lure FDIs," says Manokaran. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] China's hunger pushing prices of food commodities sharply higher; becomes source of worry domestically. Annual grain demand likely to reach 573 million tons in a decade, above current production levels. Marginal crop yields shrinking, arable land harder to find; market bets that Beijing may swiftly become more reliant on global food markets. Chicago soybeans at 14-month high largely on Chinese demand; Malaysian palm oil at 27-month peak this week; Thai rice near 6-month high. China cooking oil, sugar prices up 10%-13% this month. Inflation still well below 12-year high of 8.7% in early 2008, but price gains back in force. Back then, Beijing had to impose price caps on food to bring inflation to an even keel. Bitter medicine, but with world betting on richer, hungrier giant, government knows tough job of controlling inflation is back. See =HEARD ON THE STREET: Hot Potato In China's Rising Food Costs (chuin-wei.yap@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0737 GMT [Dow Jones] A bold 50 basis point rate hike, to 4.50%, is justified in Poland, Soignee says. "We think it possible that the MPC will go for a bold 50bp hike," the bank says. Notes the MPC seems to have changed their communication strategy recently and the lack of comments from MPC members in the media makes it more difficult to understand the bias of each member and hence forecast the potential interest rates changes. "In our view, even if they don't go for a 50bp hike today, we are still confident that they will pull the trigger at the next meeting", SocGen says. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0734 GMT [Dow Jones] With Ireland away from the capital markets for now, Portuguese and Spanish auctions are the ones to monitor for investor appetite for semi-stressed periphery, says ING strategist Padhraic Garvey ahead of Portugal's up to EUR1.25 billion bond auction Wednesday. "That said both markets have done better of late, and we maintain our view that auction pressure has now eased, and should not become a significant market focus until we get to the 2011 auction program," he says. Padhraic adds that optically the 2018-dated bond looks a better value but the 2014-dated one has been "in greater favor" in recent trading days.(emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0734 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts track bunds lower Wednesday, with the latter slumping as stop losses get triggered. Gilts had already been pummelled on Tuesday as hopes of the BOE reviving quantitative easing in November faded after strong 3Q GDP data. "There is a technical hue to this selling as we were close to some important levels in schatz and bunds and it seems some stops have gone through," says Orlando Green at Credit Agricole. December gilts are down 0.63 at 122.87, falling to their lowest in a month. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT [Dow Jones] There is more to the sharp fall in the AUD than just a slightly softer than expected Australian CPI. "The AUD slide shows how much this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is held as the proxy for high yield and emerging Asia, and its sharp fall is indicative of a nervous market approaching the FOMC next week," says RBS, noting the WSJ story that the Fed will opt for "a few hundred" billion dollars of Treasury purchases rather than the 'shock and awe' approach. AUD/USD trades at 0.9698, from around 0.9855 late in the New York session Tuesday. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com) [ 10-27-10 0357ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101027e6ar00052\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
