\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Down; Off Lows On Suspected  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 03:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0137 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB falls to 29.84 vs 29.87 late yesterday in tandem with broad USD weakness on selling by exporters, interbank players. Singapore MAS's surprise tightening move also partially boosts THB sentiment. Pair off 29.80 early low on suspected USD buying by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 29.80 and above, say 2 dealers. "The bank is estimated to have bought over a hundred million dollar this morning. So long as it stays, I don't think other players in the market would be eager to sell dollar aggressively," says one. Market cautious about potential introduction of additional measures to curb inflows flagged by authorities though most don't expect any draconian moves in near future. Pair expected in 29.80-29.90 band today. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI breaks key 1500 psychological barrier, +0.3% at 1501.00, highest since mid January; dealers say combination of bullish leads contributing to KLCI's rise, including gains in overseas markets, positive sentiment ahead of release of 2011 budget tomorrow, rally in palm oil prices. "Given the positive environment, both externally and on the domestic front, the KLCI looks set to challenge the 1524 level soon, which is the all-time high," dealer says. Speculation PM Najib may call early polls in 2011 may also drive KLCI higher, says OSK Research, which raises 2011 KLCI target to 1648 from 1580 previously. Among gainers, \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +4.0% at MYR19.34 on higher palm oil prices, Manulife (1058.KU) +12.0% at MYR3.18 after major shareholder made offer to buy out rest of shares in insurer. Among decliners, Unisem (5005.KU) down 0.5% at MYR2.03 on profit taking after rallying 7% in past 3 days. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher, taking cue from overseas markets' gains; bellweather PSEi likely to rise above 4200 after closing +0.7% to 4194.06 on Wednesday; "We're hoping that market will consolidate to build a base before surging higher. But the bias is still favoring a stronger market," says Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities. Property developers and utilities such as Ayala Land (ALI.PH), Megaworld (MEG.PH) and \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH) likely to lead market advance; PSEi support pegged at 4150, resistance 4250.(cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares running higher, led by banks, PC stocks; Taiex +1.2% at 8203.15, but meets strong resistance at 8210. Cathay Financial (2882.TW) +1.1% at NT$47.20, Hua Nan (2880.TW) +0.5% at NT$20.10; both banking units win approvals to set up 1st branches in China yesterday. CLSA analyst Bruce Warden remains positive on Taiwan banks over next 12 months; "we expect to see continued gains in bank revenues and profitability, and the banks should also benefit from rising loan growth and the first steps in China." Quanta (2382.TW) +3.0% at NT$49.60, after \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  tips it to win iPad 2 orders from Apple (AAPL); other PC stocks also up, with Acer (2353.TW) +1.4% at NT$79.30, Asustek (2357.TW) +0.7% at NT$214.50. HTC (2498.TW) rebounds from yesterday's 4.3% fall, last up 0.9% at NT$675.00. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of Korea keeps benchmark interest rate steady at 2.25%, pausing in tightening cycle for 3rd straight month amid increased uncertainty about recovery in global economy, rising tensions over \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. Market had been sharply divided over BOK action; 9 of 17 economists in Dow Jones poll tipped BOK to hike by 25 bps, with remaining forecasting no move. Analysts say uncertain global economic outlook, rising KRW likely to prompt BOK to raise rates in cautious, measured way ahead, or pause at least until 1Q 2011; "external conditions aren't likely to allow the BOK to pursue its monetary tightening aggressively. I don't expect any hikes in the next couple of months," says KTB Securities economist Lee Seung-soo. Players' focus squarely on BOK's detailed statement later, followed by governor's briefing at 0220 GMT, for hints on next move. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI likely to rise, may test 24,000 in short term, if not this week, as HK market's momentum accelerating, says futures trader; cites 2 examples to illustrate risk-on sentiment: first, Standard Chartered's (2888.HK) unexpected rights issue announcement yesterday which "failed to impact the sentiment even a bit"; second, sizable gains in blue chips suggest fund managers, not retail investors, chasing shares higher. Blue chips Belle (1880.HK), Ping An (2318.HK), \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  (0293.HK), \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) each up at least 5% Wednesday, all without company-specific newsflow. Cnooc (0883.HK) likely to resume uptrend today after 2-day pullback, HK developers may gain after profit-taking yesterday. HSI ends +1.5%, at more than 2-year high of 23,457.69 yesterday, up 7 of past 8 sessions and 24 out of 29 overall; volume robust at HK$109.24 billion. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares building on yesterday's firm momentum on hopes of stronger demand for SGD-denominated assets following MAS move to tighten monetary policy. STI +0.5% at 3217.38, with resistance eyed at 3254 (peak at May 20, 2008). "We see a mild positive for the STI, as foreign investment funds will flow in anticipation of an appreciating Singapore dollar," says DMG. Market breadth at 5 gainers for every decliner. Lower liners most actively traded, led by \uc2 United Fiber System\uc2  (P30.SG), +8.3% at S$0.065, \uc2 Jaya Holdings\uc2  (J10.SG), +8.8% at S$0.74, Ban Joo (B07.SG), flat at S$0.04. Among blue chips, notable gainers include CityDev (C09.SG), +3.3% at S$13.74, Wilmar (F34.SG), +2.0% at S$6.27. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR steady at 8,920 as Bank Indonesia suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support pair, which remains under selling pressure, partly linked to Singapore's surprise monetary tightening. 3 dealers say Bank Indonesia bought in an order of $20 million at 8,920, a level it has been defending in recent days. Pair tipped in narrow 8,915-8,930 range for day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] OSK Research raises 2011 KLCI target to 1648 from 1580 previously. Says latest move by government to postpone implementation of GST may be precursor to PM Najib calling for an early general election in 2011. Any general election would, in turn, likely be preceded by slew of infrastructure development projects. "As such, sentiment on the ground should remain positive and the good news may help spur further rallies in the stock market for 2011." KLCI target raised to reflect higher PE valuation of 16X vs 15X previously. KLCI last +0.3% at 1501.25. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude extends gains in electronic trading as EUR up vs USD following overnight gains on strong China crude import data. "More speculative money is flowing into commodities prompted by the weak dollar, and investors are not paying much attention to high U.S. oil inventories," says Koichi Murakami, analyst with broker Daiichi Shohin. Adds, U.S. Energy Information Administration's weekly oil inventory data, due later today, won't dent positive sentiment unless expectation of 1.2 million-barrel build exceeded. November Nymex crude last up 72 cents at $83.73/bbl on Globex after rising 1.6% overnight. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF will offer Y600 billion worth of 30-year JGBs later in day with expected coupon at 2.0% vs 2.0% previously; RBS Securities strategist RuiXue Xu expects firm demand due to "steady buying from absolute yield buyers" and even if result turns out to be weaker than expected, "it will likely provide a good opportunity for dip buyers." Says demand in super-long end tends to improve in 2H of fiscal year as life insurance firms typically increase buying around this time of year. Lead December JGB futures now down 0.07 at 143.84, 10-year yield last up 1.5 bps at 0.890%, 30-year yield at 1.970%, up 3.5 bps. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] Impact of latest housing measures announced by Hong Kong government Wednesday may be mild in near term, but right direction in longer-term, says Hang Seng Bank. Notes Hong Kong Chief Executive Donald Tsang unveiled plans to restrict immigration based on real-estate investments and pledged to maintain adequate land supply, providing about 61,000 new units in next 3-4 years. "Given that the ultra-low interest rate and liquidity rich environment are expected to stay for longer, the government's present policy may not be able to reverse the price trend in the near-term," report says; "but new policies to provide steady long term land and housing supply are a step in the right direction."(chester.yung@dowjones.com) [ 10-13-10 2137ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
