\par\par \b \uc2 SE Asia Stocks-Up on commodity-led buying; KL sets 33-month high\b0\par\par\uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 12:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * S'pore posts biggest gain in 4 mths; Thai index near 1,000\par \par \uc2 * Philippines, Indonesia claw back to near records\par \par \uc2 * Palm oil, energy shares lead regional gains\par \par \uc2 By Viparat Jantraprap\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Oct 13 (Reuters) - Southeast Asian stock markets gained on Wednesday amid expectations of fresh U.S. economic stimulus, with rising crude oil prices swelling demand for heavily weighted commodity shares across the region.\par \par \uc2 Share markets recouped recent losses as growing risk appetite lured more inflows into equities, although that pushed up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which made investors wary about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves by the authorities.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Straits Times Index <.FTSTI> rose 1.7 percent, its biggest one-day rise in almost four months, Malaysia <.KLSE> gained 0.7 percent, setting a 33-month high, and Indonesia <.JKSE> jumped 1.8 percent, moving near a record hit last week.\par \par \uc2 Thailand <.SETI> climbed 1.6 percent, approaching the 1,000 mark last seen before the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis, while the Philippines <.PSI> ended up 0.7 percent. Vietnam <.VNI> rose 0.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Asian and European shares in general rose on increasing expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve will ease policy to support the economy and following a positive fourth-quarter forecast from computer chipmaker Intel.\par \par \uc2 The dollar remained weak after details of the last meeting of the U.S. Federal Reserve suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was closer to injecting fresh stimulus into the ailing economy. [ID:nN12191658]\par \par \uc2 By 0938 GMT, the MSCI Asia ex-Japan index <.MIAPJ0000PUS> was up 1.2 percent, led by a 2.3 percent gain in MSCI's India index <.MIIN00000PIN> and a 2.04 percent rise in Singapore <.MISG00000PSG>.\par \par \uc2 In Singapore, the local index hovered around 28-month highs, led by palm oil companies, with \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  jumping 9.2 percent and \uc2 Indofood Agri Resources\uc2  climbing 7.9 percent amid firm palm oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  rose 2.5 percent while Indonesia's \uc2 Astra Agro Lestari\uc2  gained 3.2 percent and Thailand's top energy firm, PTT , rose over 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 In Thailand, the imposition of a 15 percent withholding tax on interest payments and capital gains earned by foreign investors on Thai bonds did little to affect stock market sentiment, dealers said. [ID:nSGE69B0A6]\par \par \uc2 Bangkok-based Sukit Udomsirikul, a strategist at broker SCB Securities, said the measures had no significant impact on stocks or bond markets. But he said investors were still cautious over further possible measures to deal with the rising baht.\par \par \uc2 "The policy interest rate may be maintained at an upcoming meeting on Oct. 20 to help ease inflow pressure as the baht tends to appreciate further," he added. (Editing by Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-SOUTHEASTASIA-STOCKS/|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|SI|IF|PHD|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101013e6ad000pa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
