\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Region's  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  To Pull Back 4Q On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman  \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \uc2 857 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 07:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will pull back this quarter as central banks step up their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helping the dollar pause in its steep slide, analysts predict.\par \par \uc2 After sharp rallies across the Asia region last month, only the Chinese yuan and the South Korean won will keep rising through December--although at a slower pace--according to a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell against the 10 main regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September to long-term or, in the case of Singapore, record lows. This prompted the region's central banks to be more aggressive in selling their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the greenback, especially after Japan returned to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in more than six years.\par \par \uc2 With Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega proclaiming that an "international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" has begun and International Monetary Fund Managing Director Dominique Strauss-Kahn warning that large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a growing concern, the aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are expected to give the swooning dollar a temporary respite.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones poll forecasts on average that the dollar will fall just 0.6% against the yuan this quarter, slowing from the 1.7% decline of last month when Beijing accelerated its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. Against the won, the dollar is tipped to fall 1.6%, adding to September's 4.9% swoon.\par \par \uc2 But against the rest of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the greenback is expected to get a lift this quarter.\par \par \uc2 In the most dramatic turnaround, the dollar is forecast to make its biggest bounce, 2.0%, against the Indonesian rupiah, against which it logged the biggest September drop, at 5.3%. Asia's other best performers this year, the Malaysian ringgit and the Thai baht, are also expected to correct in the fourth quarter as their economies slow after surprisingly strong rebounds in the first half.\par \par \uc2 Aside from the yuan, which was essentially pegged to the dollar for two years until June, most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened significantly during the 2008-2009 financial crisis as investors fled risky assets. They have risen sharply as the global economy recovered, but the region's policymakers now want to curb the rises in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect export-reliant growth as the global economy slows.\par \par \uc2 "In recent weeks, some Southeast Asia authorities appear to have become more concerned about local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, given the sharp rally in regional real effective exchange rates," analysts at Standard Chartered said, noting that stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the area is a rising possibility.\par \par \uc2 All the strategists polled expect the yuan to keep strengthening through year end, with several cutting their dollar/yuan forecasts after last month's faster move.\par \par \uc2 China is under increasing political pressure from the U.S., with President Barack Obama joining the call for a stronger yuan and the House of Representatives this week passing a bill to penalize China for allegedly keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervalued. This should compel Beijing to keep the yuan on a rising trend into next year, the strategists say.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the won--one of the worst Asian performers this year and among the few regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that haven't recovered to pre-2008 levels--has recently been playing catch-up. The Bank of Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly, local traders say, but it still has more leeway to allow appreciation than some of its fellow central banks around Asia.\par \par \uc2 Following are the latest forecasts for key U.S. dollar/Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs, as of the end of December and March, compared with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels at the end of September and the pair's performance during the month. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par End-Quarter Forecasts & September Performance Of Key Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par USD Vs            Monthly    End-Sep   Dec F'Cast  March F'Cast \par Chinese Yuan      -1.70%      6.6905      6.653       6.607 \par Hong Kong Dollar  -0.20%      7.7594      7.767       7.761 \par Korean Won        -4.90%       1,140      1,122       1,117 \par Taiwan Dollar     -2.60%       31.19      31.52       31.51 \par Singapore Dollar  -3.00%      1.3143     1.3362      1.3297 \par Malaysian Ringgit -2.00%       3.086       3.11        3.09 \par Thai Baht         -2.90%       30.34      30.90       30.94 \par Philippine Peso   -3.20%       43.88      44.07       43.90 \par Indonesian Rupiah -1.20%       8,920      8,929       8,890 \par Indian Rupiah     -5.30%       44.56      45.45       45.28 \par 