\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Malaysian Ringgit May Sail Past  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Threat\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 09:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The ringgit, Asia's best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year, may rise further ahead of key milestones for Malaysia's economic reform plans -- as long as the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sticks to its policy of not curtailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.\par \par \uc2 The ringgit is up 11% against the U.S. dollar since January, the result of strong foreign inflows to an economy growing at an 8.9% annual pace in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the ringgit may receive additional good news next month. The government is expected to present its 2011 annual budget on Oct. 15, including measures that top officials say will lower the deficit significantly from an estimated 5.6% of gross domestic product this year -- one of Asia's highest levels and a significant concern for investors.\par \par \uc2 Also in October, Petronas Petrochemicals Group Bhd., a unit of Malaysia's government-controlled oil and gas company \uc2 Petronas\uc2 , is slated to complete the country's largest initial public offering, surpassing last year's $3.6 billion IPO from \uc2 Maxis Bhd\uc2  (6012.KU).\par \par \uc2 That may be followed by more IPOs later this year, as Malaysia's government implements the New Economic Model it announced in April to boost economic growth.\par \par \uc2 This is a favorable environment for the ringgit, which on Wednesday hit MYR3.0800 to the dollar, its highest level since the 1997-98 Asian crisis. That followed news that index provider FTSE had re-classified Malaysia as an advanced emerging economy, opening the door for inflows of up to $2.4 billion from managers of indexed funds over the next year, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  estimates.\par \par \uc2 BarCap last week raised its ringgit forecasts and now expects the dollar to trade at MYR2.92 in a year's time, versus a previous forecast of MYR3.05. Some analysts anticipate even faster gains, with the dollar possibly falling to MYR3.0 as early as December.\par \par \uc2 Standard Bank economist Patrick Bennett recently noted that Malaysia, whose main commodity exports are denominated in dollars, may refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to cap ringgit rallies. That would be a stark contrast to the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in East Asian economies that export mainly manufactured products.\par \par \uc2 Bennett said this suggests the dollar could fall next year toward MYR2.80 -- a level unseen since the height of the Asian crisis.\par \par \uc2 "The government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been accommodating of ringgit gains and have no reason to turn back now," Bennett said.\par \par \uc2 Kuala Lumpur traders say Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks around MYR3.10. But recent data show any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has likely been in tiny amounts aimed at smoothing out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, rather than putting a floor beneath the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as Japan did earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Recent data show that as of Sept. 15, Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had risen just 0.7% since the end of August, to $95.9 billion. The small increase is likely explained by the higher valuation of Malaysia's euro-denominated reserves, given the euro's 2.6% rise against the dollar in the period, and implies that few if any dollars were purchased.\par \par \uc2 (David Roman, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 29-09-10 0750GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100929e69t000mg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
