\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: 10-Yr JGB Yield Won't Slide On Tankan-Strategist\b0\par\par\uc2 1,369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 04:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] 10-year JGB yield likely won't slide even if BOJ's quarterly tankan business sentiment survey due Wednesday highlights concern over economic slowdown ahead, as slower growth already priced into JGB market, says Jun Ishii, chief fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "The benchmark yield has already factored in an economic plateau and is declining accordingly." For that reason, tankan, also industrial production data for August due Thursday "are probably not going to spur further yield declines," Ishii says. While headline diffusion index of tankan expected to rise to 6 from previous survey's 1, according to Dow Jones survey, economists say report could show companies expect sentiment to fall back later in year, particular if JPY strength persists. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0232 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.8% at 9529.79 midday; trading volume low (just 584 million shares) as investors await quarterly BOJ tankan survey result due Wednesday. "It is difficult to take risks and buy exporter shares before the tankan release," says Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \uc2 Mito Securities\uc2 , noting focus on firms' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate assumptions and outlook for employment and capital spending. Puts afternoon Nikkei range at 9470-9570. 29/33 Topix subindexes lower, with pharmaceutical subindex worst performer (down 2.4%) as many shares turn ex-dividend; \uc2 Takeda Pharmaceutical\uc2  (4502.TO) down 3.6% at Y3,885, Eisai (4523.TO) down 3.8% at Y2,988. Consumer lender Takefuji (8564.TO) ask-only (limit-down) at Y116 with company expected to file for bankruptcy protection later today. Real estate shares outperform on expectations for more BOJ monetary easing; Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 0.8% at Y1,370. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI flat at 22,338.01, after opening lower (early low at 22,308.96), as DJIA's modest fall doesn't provide direction for local market; index expected to remain in tight range today. Taifook says "a pullback looks overdue" after HSI's recent gains (+8.8% month-to-yesterday), but adds "a pullback by local property stocks looks more imminent with their significant outperformance of late against the broader market." Hang Seng Property Subindex +14.4% month-to-yesterday. Adds, there may be jitters ahead of HK chief executive's policy address early October in light of possible policies to cool housing market. Among HK property stocks, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) down 0.9% at HK$129.30, \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK) down 0.4% at HK$35.65. Worst blue-chip performer \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) down 4.1% at HK$11.22 after news of US$1.839 billion convertible bonds issue. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY slightly higher after PBOC sets USD/CNY central parity well above 6.7000 psychological level for 2nd day; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.6941 vs 6.6923 last close, tipped at 6.6880-6.7000 for session. "The relatively stable fixing means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking a break from yuan's sharp appreciation trend recently," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank; but adds "people in the market are unwilling to long dollars because they expect the yuan will continue to appreciate in the longer term." Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY at 6.5755/6.5795, up from 6.5739/6.5779 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up slightly as PBOC keeps bill yields unchanged in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , easing concerns about possible tightening measures to curb inflation, says Beijing-based dealer with local insurer; notes PBOC sells CNY5 billion 1-year bills at 2.0929%, CNY6 billion worth of 3-month bills at 1.5704%, both yields flat with rates on bills sold last week. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.53 vs 126.50 last close, tipped to consolidate in narrow 126.5-126.7 band before week-long National Day holiday starts Friday. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi off 0.3% at 1855.66, with market taking a breather in absence of positive cues to generate momentum. \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Lee Jae-mahn says market appears to be in a wait-and-see mode ahead of key domestic data like August Industrial Production Index, September CPI due later this week; tips 1850 support. "The market has risen sharply during the recent sessions, so there appears to be some profit-taking now in reaction to that." \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  off 3.7% at KRW155,500 after rising 6.3% last week, while \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  (000720.SE) also down 1.3% at KRW73,700 on profit-taking pressure. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan agricultural name offering good amounts of USD/JPY this morning, says Singapore-based dealer. USD/JPY last 84.23 vs 84.18 morning low. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] 10-year JGB yield marks 1-month low at 0.960% amid mounting expectations for further BOJ easing, with JGBs also supported by strength in U.S. Treasurys overnight. 10-year JGB yield likely to stay under pressure in near term, settling "in a range centered on 1%," says Makoto Yamashita, chief Japan interest rate strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . Given speculation Fed may also conduct more quantitative easing, says "we believe it will be difficult to sell bonds as long as increasing outright government bond purchases remain a future option for both central banks." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely slightly lower as moderate fall on Wall Street Monday may inspire profit taking after recent sharp gains, traders say; tip range at 3440-3480 after shares yesterday closed +2.1% at 3468.036. "I think the market may consolidate amid an absence of fresh leads from the domestic economic front," says trader at Lautandahana Securities. Car maker Astra (ASII.JK), Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) may leads falls on profit taking after gaining more than 5% in previous 2 sessions. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares last down 0.2% at 8174.39 early as investors stay cautious ahead of heavy data week for both China, U.S., says Taiwan International equity analyst Hanna Chang. "Taiex will continue to be trapped in consolidation mode this week with an upper limit of 8,250 while notable downside risks will rest with the economic data due out this week including China's and U.S. PMI." Adds techs remain a major drag on overall market as strong TWD may hurt export orders. HTC (2498.TW) down 0.4% at NT$719.00, Acer (2353.TW) down 0.8% at NT$75.70. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop U.S. dollar after USD/SGD falls to new record low overnight, person familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking tells Dow Jones. Adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 1.3204 level aimed at smoothing USD fall. "They (MAS) can't change the trend of the weak dollar against Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but they will try to make the fall orderly. At this point they will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around S$1.3200," person says. Pair currently at 1.3222 after touching 1.3192 overnight. (djn.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher early, keeping in step with USD's advance vs some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , losses on Wall Street overnight; pair last at 43.95 in light volume after settling at 43.90 late Monday, with support expected at 43.80 and resistance at 44.20. Local bank trader notes pattern of pair falling later in session lately, positive investor sentiment for PHP given recent inflows into equities, and says: "The market is also wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially if the dollar slides to the year-low PHP43.88," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 09-27-10 2235ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
