\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Govt Bonds Up; PBOC Bill Yields Unmoved\b0\par\par\uc2 1,197 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 04:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up slightly as PBOC keeps bill yields unchanged in open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , easing concerns about possible tightening measures to curb inflation, says Beijing-based dealer with local insurer; notes PBOC sells CNY5 billion 1-year bills at 2.0929%, CNY6 billion worth of 3-month bills at 1.5704%, both yields flat with rates on bills sold last week. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.53 vs 126.50 last close, tipped to consolidate in narrow 126.5-126.7 band before week-long National Day holiday starts Friday. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi off 0.3% at 1855.66, with market taking a breather in absence of positive cues to generate momentum. \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Lee Jae-mahn says market appears to be in a wait-and-see mode ahead of key domestic data like August Industrial Production Index, September CPI due later this week; tips 1850 support. "The market has risen sharply during the recent sessions, so there appears to be some profit-taking now in reaction to that." \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  off 3.7% at KRW155,500 after rising 6.3% last week, while \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  (000720.SE) also down 1.3% at KRW73,700 on profit-taking pressure. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan agricultural name offering good amounts of USD/JPY this morning, says Singapore-based dealer. USD/JPY last 84.23 vs 84.18 morning low. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] 10-year JGB yield marks 1-month low at 0.960% amid mounting expectations for further BOJ easing, with JGBs also supported by strength in U.S. Treasurys overnight. 10-year JGB yield likely to stay under pressure in near term, settling "in a range centered on 1%," says Makoto Yamashita, chief Japan interest rate strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . Given speculation Fed may also conduct more quantitative easing, says "we believe it will be difficult to sell bonds as long as increasing outright government bond purchases remain a future option for both central banks." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely slightly lower as moderate fall on Wall Street Monday may inspire profit taking after recent sharp gains, traders say; tip range at 3440-3480 after shares yesterday closed +2.1% at 3468.036. "I think the market may consolidate amid an absence of fresh leads from the domestic economic front," says trader at Lautandahana Securities. Car maker Astra (ASII.JK), Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) may leads falls on profit taking after gaining more than 5% in previous 2 sessions. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares last down 0.2% at 8174.39 early as investors stay cautious ahead of heavy data week for both China, U.S., says Taiwan International equity analyst Hanna Chang. "Taiex will continue to be trapped in consolidation mode this week with an upper limit of 8,250 while notable downside risks will rest with the economic data due out this week including China's and U.S. PMI." Adds techs remain a major drag on overall market as strong TWD may hurt export orders. HTC (2498.TW) down 0.4% at NT$719.00, Acer (2353.TW) down 0.8% at NT$75.70. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop U.S. dollar after USD/SGD falls to new record low overnight, person familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking tells Dow Jones. Adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 1.3204 level aimed at smoothing USD fall. "They (MAS) can't change the trend of the weak dollar against Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but they will try to make the fall orderly. At this point they will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around S$1.3200," person says. Pair currently at 1.3222 after touching 1.3192 overnight. (djn.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher early, keeping in step with USD's advance vs some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , losses on Wall Street overnight; pair last at 43.95 in light volume after settling at 43.90 late Monday, with support expected at 43.80 and resistance at 44.20. Local bank trader notes pattern of pair falling later in session lately, positive investor sentiment for PHP given recent inflows into equities, and says: "The market is also wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially if the dollar slides to the year-low PHP43.88," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] September HSI futures down 0.2% at 22,306, suggesting any profit-taking pressure won't be significant, even after HSI up in 11 out of last 12 sessions. Contract ends yesterday at 20-point premium to cash while October futures end at 19-point premium, continuing to flag positive bias in market's near-term direction. Rollover may accelerate ahead of September futures' expiry tomorrow, with open interest still 81,049 lots, October's at 63,445. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. bank sold $150 million around Y84.25 in past 10 minutes, purportedly option-related, says Singapore-based dealer. USD/JPY last 84.25. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares slightly down early as property developers, banks slide on concerns over further tightening measures for real estate sector. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.3% at 2619.37, tipped to stay close to 2600 for now. "It seems that the market has mostly digested any potential impact of further tightening measures for real estate developers. Investors are just waiting for the other shoe to drop now," says Huang Bin at Gold State Securities. Tips new measures likely to be rolled out after National Day holiday October 1-7, may bring some relief for property sector. Among actives, \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) down 1.4% at CNY8.01, Gemdale (600383.SH) down 0.7% at CNY6.11. ICBC down 0.5% at CNY3.98. Shenzhen index down 0.3% at 1167.52. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares may open higher on positive momentum from yesterday when SET index hit fresh 14-year high. However, profit-taking may emerge, while regional weakness likely to weigh on market sentiment. "I think the Thai stock market would be volatile...and investors may lock in profits after the gains. But the stock market remains in an upward trend on persistent capital inflows," says analyst with Far East Securities Parin Kitchatornpitak. Notes, MOF yesterday revised up Thailand's 2010 GDP growth forecast to 7.5% vs 5.5%. Investors eying planned protest by environment activists in Map Ta Phut area against list of 11 activities--recently approved by Cabinet--deemed to be harmful. Near-term resistance tipped at 965, then 970, support at 960-955 zone. SET index yesterday ended +1.1% at 962.47. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com) [ 09-27-10 2217ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s0001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
