o the year-low PHP43.88," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] September HSI futures down 0.2% at 22,306, suggesting any profit-taking pressure won't be significant, even after HSI up in 11 out of last 12 sessions. Contract ends yesterday at 20-point premium to cash while October futures end at 19-point premium, continuing to flag positive bias in market's near-term direction. Rollover may accelerate ahead of September futures' expiry tomorrow, with open interest still 81,049 lots, October's at 63,445. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. bank sold $150 million around Y84.25 in past 10 minutes, purportedly option-related, says Singapore-based dealer. USD/JPY last 84.25. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares slightly down early as property developers, banks slide on concerns over further tightening measures for real estate sector. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.3% at 2619.37, tipped to stay close to 2600 for now. "It seems that the market has mostly digested any potential impact of further tightening measures for real estate developers. Investors are just waiting for the other shoe to drop now," says Huang Bin at Gold State Securities. Tips new measures likely to be rolled out after National Day holiday October 1-7, may bring some relief for property sector. Among actives, \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) down 1.4% at CNY8.01, Gemdale (600383.SH) down 0.7% at CNY6.11. ICBC down 0.5% at CNY3.98. Shenzhen index down 0.3% at 1167.52. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares may open higher on positive momentum from yesterday when SET index hit fresh 14-year high. However, profit-taking may emerge, while regional weakness likely to weigh on market sentiment. "I think the Thai stock market would be volatile...and investors may lock in profits after the gains. But the stock market remains in an upward trend on persistent capital inflows," says analyst with Far East Securities Parin Kitchatornpitak. Notes, MOF yesterday revised up Thailand's 2010 GDP growth forecast to 7.5% vs 5.5%. Investors eying planned protest by environment activists in Map Ta Phut area against list of 11 activities--recently approved by Cabinet--deemed to be harmful. Near-term resistance tipped at 965, then 970, support at 960-955 zone. SET index yesterday ended +1.1% at 962.47. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0143 GMT [Dow Jones] HK blue chips appear set to face broad-based profit-taking, with 33 of 45 index members bid down at pre-open. HK developers, HKEx (0388.HK), Hutchison (0013.HK) among most overbought stocks recently; New World Development (0017.HK) bid down 2.0% at HK$14.70, HKEx bid down 1.1% at HK$151.30, Hutchison indicated off 2.1% at HK$66.05. \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) worst performer so far at pre-open, quoted 2.6% lower to HK$11.40, weighed by news of US$1.839 billion convertible bonds issue. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares barely ruffled by pullback on Wall Street, with STI +0.2% at 3121.04. Resistance expected at 3146, higher end of breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "The Singapore economy is simply not so exposed to what happens in the G-3 (U.S., EU, Japan) any more," says Phillip Securities strategist Joshua Tan. Notes G-3 markets account for only a third of Singapore's non-oil domestic exports; "with two-thirds of the world firing away, it's hard not to have a positive secular outlook. We are likely to see funds hedge against a slow G-3 by buying Asia, and among that, overweighting the STI." Among large caps, \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG) +2.0% at S$2.06, SembMarine (S51.SG) +1.3% at S$3.95. Small caps actively traded, with Thai Beverage (Y92.SG), flat at S$0.285, Informatics (I03.SG), +2.9% at S$0.18, Top Global (519.SG) flat at S$0.01. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped lower, likely taking cues from losses on Wall Street; market also likely prone to profit taking after closing yesterday at new record high 4122.82, up 1.1%. Benchmark PSEi may find support at 4050, while resistance pegged at 4200. "We're due for profit-taking. However, sentiment is still bullish, so we should close off the lows," says Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities; likely to lead market retreat is blue chip PLDT (TEL.PH), whose ADRs down in NY overnight. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD higher at 7.7585 vs 7.7578 late yesterday on stronger USD offshore, says senior trader at U.S. bank. Tips pair in 7.7570-7.7610 band near term. "Some market players are buying the U.S. dollar on bargain-hunting...still, the pair remain biased down as the recent strength of local stocks could continue to attract capital inflows" trader says. HSI up 1% Monday, rising for 6th straight session (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may fall at open, track modest fall in Dow Jones Industrial Average, profit-taking also likely after index's 8.8% rise month-to-yesterday, with index also hitting fresh 8-month high of 22,407 Monday. "It's set to give back some of its recent gains, but I don't expect strong selling pressure, as the overall trading sentiment remains positive," says Conita Hung, Head of Research at Delta Asia. Tips index in 22,200-22,400 range today. Expects HK property stocks may take breather after recent rally, while \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) set to fall after news it's raising US$1.8 billion from convertible bond sale. HSI ended +1.0% at 22,340.84 Monday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.3% at 1460.96, following weaker U.S. stocks overnight. "Since there aren't any strong leads at the moment, investors are content to take profits from yesterday's rise," dealer says, referring to KLCI's 0.9% gain yesterday; tips key index to remain in narrow 1455-1465 range rest of day. Stocks up sharply yesterday among leading decliners so far today, with AirAsia (5099.KU) down 1.8% at MYR2.21, Dayang (5141.KU) down 1.8% at MYR2.20. \uc2 BAT Malaysia\uc2  (4162.KU) down 1.0% at MYR48.00 on concerns cigarette taxes will be raised in 2011 government budget to be unveiled October 15. Among gainers, Taliwork (8524.KU) +4.7% at MYR1.34 after clinching 30-year water treatment concession in China. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 09-27-10 2156ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
