\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 846 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =ADB RAISES ASIAN 2010 GROWTH FORECAST BUT FLAGS CHINA SLOWDOWN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Asian Development Bank Tuesday raised its forecasts for Asian economic growth this year, citing the region's unexpectedly strong rebound from the global crisis, but left its forecast for China unchanged in anticipation of a continued slowdown in Asia's largest economy. (published 0200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ADB: YUAN APPRECIATION IS IN CHINA'S AND WORLD'S INTERESTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian Development Bank Chief Economist Jong-Wha Lee said Tuesday it is in the interests of both China and the world for China to allow its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to appreciate and increase exchange-rate flexibility, as a stronger yuan will help to strengthen the country's household consumption power. (published 0327 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN METI MINISTER: INVESTIGATING IMPACT OF SLOWING IMPORTS FROM CHINA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan is investigating the impact on its domestic companies of slowing imports from China, aiming to gather sufficient facts to encourage China to resolve any problems in the trade flow, Minister of Economy, Trade and Industry Akihiro Ohata said Tuesday. (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAS BRIEFLY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO CUSHION US DOLLAR FALL -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Tuesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar hit an all-time high against the greenback, a person familiar with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's thinking said. (published 0143 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA PLANS TOTAL SPENDING OF KRW309.6 TLN IN 2011, UP 5.7% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government unveiled Tuesday a budget plan for next year that aims at fiscal prudence as the local economy recovers fully from the worst global economic downturn in decades. (published 0200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: S KOREA SEPTEMBER CONSUMER SENTIMENT INDEX AT 14-MONTH LOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A key index measuring consumer confidence in South Korea fell for a second straight month to a 14-month low in September, reflecting rising concerns about a possible slowdown in the pace of recovery in the domestic economy. (published 0309 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA OFFICIAL: 2010 INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT GROWTH COULD HIT 13%-14% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's year-on-year industrial output growth for 2010 could be 13%-14%, said Xin Guobin, head of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  monitoring coordination division at the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology, according to remarks posted on the ministry's website Monday. (published 0133 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINESE STEELMAKERS WON'T ACCEPT COSTS FROM AUSTRALIA MINING TAX-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese steelmakers won't willingly accept additional costs from a potential Australian mining tax, Reuters news agency quoted Shan Shanghua, secretary general of China Iron & Steel Association as saying Tuesday. (published 0325 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: PHILIPPINE TRADE BALANCE IN DEFICIT ON STRONG JULY IMPORTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philippine imports grew at a double-digit pace in July, reflecting strong domestic demand and economic activity and sending the trade balance back into deficit after one month in the black. (published 0459 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: VIETNAM JANUARY-SEPTEMBER GDP UP 6.52% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam's gross domestic product in the first nine months of this year grew 6.52% from a year earlier, the government said Tuesday. (published 0207 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE VISITOR ARRIVALS UP 18% ON YEAR TO 996,000 IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visitor arrivals to Singapore rose 18% on year to 996,000 in August, marking the ninth straight month of record arrivals to the island nation, according to a government statement Tuesday. (published 0433 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER: NOT DESIRABLE TO ISSUE NEW DEBT FOR EXTRA BUDGET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister said Tuesday that the government should not rely on debt issuance to finance a planned extra budget. (published 0227 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: 2-YEAR JGBS MEET SOLID DEMAND AMID BOJ EASING SPECULATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two-year Japanese government bonds saw strong demand at an auction Tuesday, as speculation that the Bank of Japan will likely further ease its monetary policy caused investors to buy the policy-sensitive notes. (published 0452 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NZ DMO TO LAUNCH GOVERNMENT BOND MATURING MARCH 15, 2019 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The NZ Debt Management Office said Tuesday that would launch a new government bond on Sept. 30, with a maturity date of March 15, 2019. (published 2203 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: \f28 \uc2 \f2 KOREA ELECTRIC POWER\f28 \uc2 \f2  PRICES $700M 5-YEAR BOND AT 99.664 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Korea Electric Power Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., South Korea's sole electric power transmission and distribution company, priced a $700 million, five-year bond deal Monday at 180 basis points over comparable U.S. Treasurys, according to people familiar with the matter. (published 0127 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par  \par =BIG PICTURE: SKIRMISHES WITH CHINA INCREASE RISK OF TRADE WAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 I fear this could end badly. China has been firing trade salvos at both Japan and the U.S. While the motivation differs in each instance, the overriding lesson to keep in the forefront is that trade wars never solved anything. (published 1927 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: YEN OPTIONS DOWN AS SPOT REMAINS QUIET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options fell Tuesday as the spot stayed quiet due to lingering speculation that any sharp moves might cause the Japanese government to again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, decreasing hedging demand as a result. (published 0316 GMT) [ 28-09-10 0500GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100928e69s0009o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
