\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: More JPY  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  May Be Coming Ahead-\uc2 Westpac\b0\par\par\uc2 1,810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2010\par 08:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] Renewed rounds of Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken JPY are likely in coming days and may even stretch for months, despite lack of recent activity, says Sean Callow, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It's ridiculous to suggest that they had a big day and then they won't do it again," he adds. "They waited for six years to act." Reckons coming \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be similar to 2003-2004 episode, with extended small-scale moves rather than the "shock and awe" action taken last week; tips Y84 as next tripwire, especially if USD/JPY slides in the absence of clear macroeconomic factors, with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  possible in early European session or even NY time, but more likely during the Asian session. USD/JPY last at 84.50 vs 84.54. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional shares mixed; S&P/ASX 200 flat, STI 0.1% lower, KLCI off 0.7%, SET +1.2%, NZX-50 down 0.3%, Taiex flat, PSEi +0.3%, Sensex off 0.3%. China, Hong Kong, Japan and South Korean markets shut for holidays. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD/JPY at 84.52 vs 84.54 late NY trade Wednesday. EUR/JPY at 113.31 vs 113.22; EUR/USD at 1.3403 vs 1.3394. USD/SGD hit all time low at 1.3243 in overnight trade Wednesday; pair last at 1.3257 vs 1.3274 late Asia trade Wednesday. China Premier Wen ruled out any large-scale appreciation in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defiance of mounting pressures from U.S. policymakers; also says CNY exchange rate issue is "economic issue" that shouldn't be "politicized" and can't resolve trade imbalances between U.S., China. India Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee says nation may surpass 8.5%-8.75% economic growth target this fiscal year through March. Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tolerating weaker USD because rises by EUR and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  limiting broad impact of SGD's rise, person familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking tells Dow Jones; nation's August CPI +3.3% on-year vs +3.1% in July, in-line with expectations. New Zealand 2Q GDP +0.2% seasonally-adjusted on-quarter vs +0.5% in 1Q, +0.8% expected; Finance Minister Bill English says nation's tepid 2Q GDP figures point to ongoing patchy recovery. Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Diwa Guinigundo says bank ready to step into \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb excessive volatility in exchange rate. Spot gold at $1,291.50/oz, up 30 cents from NY close. November Nymex crude oil futures up 5 cents at $74.75/bbl on Globex. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD sharply down on pent-up demand for TWD from foreign funds, after Taiwan markets closed yesterday for Mid-Autumn Festival holiday, says trader from foreign bank; pair last at 31.541 vs 31.765 late Tuesday, midday volume high at US$596 million. "It's all about a weakening U.S. dollar. It looks like (Taiwan's) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and exporters are trying very hard to cap the gains (of TWD) by buying the U.S. dollar in the market." Says "tricky" to estimate amount of suspected buying by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as buying by exporters; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't be reached for comment. Tips 31.5 as pair's immediate support. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.2% at 956.32 midday pause amid brisk trade. Big cap stocks buoyant on persistent capital inflows overshadowing slides in telecom plays after court earlier today upholds 3G licensing auction injunction. "Sentiment will likely continue to be positive as funds are still flowing into the stock market," says Ayudhya Securities senior VP Kasamapon Hamnilrat. Foreigners snapping up Thai shares, have been net buyers of THB9.51 billion over past three days. Resistance tipped at 960, support at 945-946. Among top actives, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +2.4% at THB696, PTT (PTT.TH) +1.0% at THB296, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) +3.1% at THB99.75, \uc2 True Corp\uc2  (TRUE.TH) off 8.9% at THB4.50, \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  (ADVANC.TH) off 0.8% at THB91. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0523 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia share down 0.5% midday at 3325.494 in moderate volume as foreigners sell blue chips after recent gains, traders say; tip support at 3310. Index still up 31% year to date. "If selling continues, the main index could test its strong support at 3300 tomorrow," says trader at Valbury Securities. Among decliners: food producer \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) down 6.1% at IDR5,400, heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) down 2.2% at IDR8,750, while coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) down 4.7% at IDR2075. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0522 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tolerating a weaker USD as gains in EUR, other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are limiting the broad impact of SGD's rise, person familiar with MAS's thinking tells Dow Jones Newswires; adds with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading basket (used by MAS to guide SGD) moving higher, MAS might move in to defend USD if USD/SGD falls to around 1.3200, which now the strong end of MAS's undisclosed policy band for SGD, down from around S$1.33 last week, S$1.34 before that. Note USD/SGD hit all time low of S$1.3243 in overnight trade yesterday in line with broad USD weakness. Pair now at 1.3266 vs 1.3274 late Asia trade Wednesday. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0501 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore August CPI rose at faster pace driven by increases in transport and housing costs. CPI +3.3% on-year, in-line with Dow Jones Newswires poll and vs +3.1% in July. CPI +0.3% on-month seasonally-adjusted, also in-line with poll and vs +0.5% in July. Increases largely due to higher car ownership levies but also some pressures in housing market. Suggests inflation still fairly well behaved in Singapore especially in relation to imported price pressures such as food, clothing, and supports views MAS unlikely to take any action at October policy meeting. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares last down 0.1% at 3233.16; "It looks like we have just followed along with the rest of the world," says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker James Porteous. Notes volumes picking up; "Activity is quite good. There is a wee bit more interest in the markets. People are seeing the underlying performance and are chasing yield." Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) +0.1% at NZ$8.61, Telecom (TEL.NZ) +0.5% at NZ$2.04. Exporters weaker as NZD remains strong; Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) down 3.2% at NZ$3.05. Kathmandu +2.9% at NZ$1.77 ahead of FY result tomorrow. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.7% at 1463.76 midday after failure to break key resistance level triggered profit-taking on recent strong performers like Genting (3182.KU). KLCI rose to 1477.03 in early trade, failing yet again to breach 1480 barrier for 3rd consecutive session; that, along with absence of fresh positive catalysts, spurred pullback amid concerns market's rally to 32-month high may be running out of steam. Dealers say KLCI to continue testing immediate support at 1462 (10-day moving average); if support breached, KLCI could extend losses to 1450 near-term. "The medium-term bullish outlook, however, remains intact as long as the KLCI sustains above the 30-day moving average, which is now at 1420," dealer says. Among decliners, Genting (3182.KU) down 4.2% at MYR9.94 after hitting fresh record high yesterday, Top Glove (7113.KU) down 4.5% at MYR5.06. Among gainers, SP Setia (8664.KU) +1.1% at MYR4.54 ahead of fiscal 3Q results later today. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stock market much quieter vs yesterday with players having few leads to follow as key Asian bourses closed, corporate newsflow thin. Overall volume 1.11 billion shares worth S$731.6 million vs Wednesday's full day 3.47 billion shares worth S$2.39 billion. STI off 0.1% at 3093.26 midday after drifting in tight 3091-3102 band in morning. Support at August 3043 high, resistance at year-to-date 3116 peak. Market breadth flat vs positive in early trade. Momentum even in lower liners has eased; "as small caps participation starts to draw more traders, bouts of profit taking or shake-outs also become more common but this is healthy as it checks euphoria," says DBS Vickers. Most active stocks in market include GMG Global (5IM.SG), +5.6% at S$0.285, UMS Holdings (558.SG), +5.4% at S$0.49, Parkway Life (C2PU.SG), off 5.4% at S$1.58, Genting Singapore (G13.SG), off 1.0% at S$2.08. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD recovering from earlier weakness following lower-than-expect NZ 2Q GDP number as USD aversion prevails, says Chris Hunter, dealer at Custom House, a \uc2 Western Union Company\uc2 ; "We are seeing a broadly weaker U.S. dollar and that is the prevailing theme on the global markets at the moment." Pair last at 0.7326 vs 0.7381 early this morning. Adds, market will be watching U.S. new housing data due out overnight for further cues; "It wouldn't surprise me if the Kiwi did keep rising... There is aversion toward the U.S. dollar which is assisting us no matter what we do in our own back yard to mess things up." Tips support at 0.7300 with resistances at 0.7390. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0425 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher with investors taking interest in second and third liners. Bellwether PSEi closed up 0.3% to 4067.43 amid heavy trading. "Foreign buyers have stopped buying in the meantime and it's the local investors who are more active with second and third liners," says Eagle Equities president Joseph Roxas. Tips market likely to consolidate as investors eye overseas cues; next support 4030, resistance 4080. Most active are SM Investments (SM.PH), off 0.6% at PHP507; Lepanto Consolidating Mining (LC.PH), +33% at PHP0.56; Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH), +11.5% at PHP66.90. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com) [ 09-23-10 0220ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100923e69n0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
