\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Japan Takes On The $4 Trillion Monster\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mark Cranfield   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 07:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Those working on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at the Bank of Japan may find themselves selling lots and lots of yen in the next 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 For Japan to succeed in breaking the yen-buying psychology of the four trillion dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , via the BOJ, may have to be willing to buy U.S. dollars on a scale it has never previously done and be prepared to fight the world's largest market, all day and all night.\par \par \uc2 For inspiration Japan can look back at its successful attack on the yen in April 1995. With dollar/yen just below 80, Japan started buying billions of dollars; traders at the time estimated it bought over twenty billion dollars in one day, starting in Tokyo and continuing into the North American trading session. It worked; within one month the yen had weakened 8% and by the end of 1995 it had weakened 23%.\par \par \uc2 However in the mid-1990s the U.S. economy was enjoying a much better time than it is today; unemployment was much lower and economic growth was on an uptrend at the start of the technology-led boom.\par \par \uc2 Contrast that with 2010 and the U.S. may not stand idly by and let Japan weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too far before it puts Japan in the dock alongside China to accuse it of manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Partly for that reason, Japan is likely to be on its own in this contest with the market; notably there are no bank economists forecasting the U.S. Treasury will join Japan as a partner to weaken the yen and strengthen the dollar this time around.\par \par \uc2 Thus-far \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have been in the range of three to five billion dollars; significantly the Bank of Japan has apparently agreed to leave the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized for now, which could help convince traders that Japan is serious about its job.\par \par \uc2 In 2010 Japan may also be satisfied with a more modest degree of success.\par \par \uc2 If the market can be persuaded to stop buying yen for long enough to question the notion that dollar/yen is a one-way bet, it may be enough to bring foreign investors back into the Japanese stock market. The slide in the Nikkei Stock Average index below the 9000 mark in late August could have been the tipping point which led to selling of yen Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Wherever the market is heading from here, the next 24 hours may be crucial for the MOF and the Bank of Japan as it carries out the MOF's instructions. Pizza delivery anyone?\par \par \uc2 (Mark Cranfield is an analyst for Dow Jones CommentaryPlus. He was previously a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond trader for major banks in London, Hong Kong & Singapore. He can be reached on 65-6415-4034 or via email: mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 15-09-10 0507GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1171 1184 1187 4253 4256 5008 70779   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100915e69f000bi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\