\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: MAS To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  If US Dollar Stays Below S$1.34010-Source\b0\par\par\uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 07:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Adds quotes, Bank Negara \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , background) \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is poised to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, selling the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the second time, this month if the U.S. dollar trades "consistently" below S$1.3410, a person familiar with the situation said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore is watching the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise "very closely," the person told Dow Jones Newswires. "If (the U.S. dollar) trades consistently below S$1.3410," the MAS "will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hit a record high Thursday, with the greenback falling to S$1.3407 as Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed on renewed global risk appetite. At 0405 GMT it was quoted at S$1.3428.\par \par \uc2 The MAS, which had largely stayed out of the market since April, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sept. 3 after the U.S. dollar fell to S$1.3425.\par \par \uc2 The person said the MAS would have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again Thursday if it wasn't for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen, the Thai baht, the Malaysian ringgit and the Korean won that make up part of the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses to guide the Singapore dollar.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar has been sliding against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, first as Asia's strong economic recovery has attracted heavy investment and, more recently, on expectations that the Federal Reserve will need to loosen U.S. monetary policy further as the world's biggest economy slows.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in export-reliant countries are growing concerned about the rises in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a growing number of Asian central banks has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to keep their exporters competitive.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  and the Bank of Korea have been steadily buying dollars and the Bank of Japan has repeatedly threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in more than six years as the yen hits 15-year highs.\par \par \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2 , which had also been standing aside since April, has begun to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the ringgit around 13-year highs, traders in Kuala Lumpur say. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars last week and as recently as Wednesday at MYR3.1110-MYR3.1120, the traders said.\par \par \uc2 As with its peers in Asia, Bank Negara appears to be trying to smooth its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise and keep moves orderly, rather than reversing the ringgit's strength. Traders expect the dollar to fall to around MYR3.1000 in the near term from MYR3.1100 early Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "This is what every Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doing," said the person familiar with MAS thinking.\par \par \uc2 "They all expect a weaker dollar, but they are trying to make the fall orderly and discourage speculation," the person said. "There are of course red lines and in Singapore, at this point, anything below S$1.34 will prompt action which will be beyond what they call 'smoothing.'"\par \par \uc2 The MAS also views the Singapore dollar's rise as part of a trend among Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after China decoupled the yuan from the U.S. dollar in mid-June, the person said. Although the yuan has barely risen, the de-pegging by the People's Bank of China paved the way for other export-reliant Asian economies to let their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gain further, the person said.\par \par \uc2 The MAS aggressively tightened policy in April by raising the trading band for the Singapore dollar and targeting a "modest and gradual appreciation" for the local dollar, changing its previous policy of zero appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's economy grew a blistering 18.8% in the second quarter from a year earlier, accelerating from 16.9% in the first quarter and after contracting last year due to the global economic crisis. The government expects the economy to grow 13%-15% for the full year.\par \par \uc2 -By Singapore Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4151; djnews.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Benjamin Low in Kuala Lumpur contributed to this report.) [ 09-09-10 0508GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100909e699000c5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
