\par\par \b \uc2 MAS Watching Singapore Dollar Rise 'Very Closely' -Source\b0\par\par\uc2 164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 03:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Corrected September 8, 2010 22:00 ET (0200 GMT) [ 09-09-10 0146GMT ]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is poised to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the local dollar trades "consistently" below S$1.3410, a person familiar with the situation said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The are watching the (local dollar's) rise very closely. If it trades consistently below S$1.3410 they will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," that person told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar briefly hit an all-time high of S$1.3407 earlier Thursday amid widespread weakness of the greenback against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At 0155 GMT it traded at S$1.3414.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sept. 3, after the U.S. dollar fell to S$1.3425, according to a person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking.\par \par \uc2 -By Singapore Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4151; djnews.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com [ 09-09-10 0201GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100909e6990004l\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs 